EXAM_DATA.papers["2005"] = {"id":"2005","year":2005,"label":"2005 旧卷","era":"old","sourceUrl":"https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/kaoyan/paper/2005/","timeLimitMin":180,"scoreMode":"raw","parts":[{"id":"section1","kind":"cloze","title":"Section I 完形填空","directions":"Directions: Read the following text. Choose the best word(s) for each numbered blank and mark A, B, C or D on the ANSWER SHEET . (10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section1-1","text":"The human nose is an underrated tool. Humans are often thought to be insensitive smellers compared with animals,   {{1}}   this is largely because,   {{2}}   animals, we stand upright. This means that our noses are   {{3}}   to perceiving those smells which float through the air,   {{4}}   the majority of smells which stick to surfaces. In fact,   {{5}}   , we are extremely sensitive to smells,   {{6}}   we do not generally realize it. Our noses are capable of   {{7}}   human smells even when these are   {{8}}   to far below one part in one million."},{"id":"p-section1-2","text":"Strangely, some people find that they can smell one type of flower but not another,   {{9}}   others are sensitive to the smells of both flowers. This may be because some people do not have the genes necessary to generate   {{10}}   smell receptors in the nose. These receptors are the cells which sense smells and send   {{11}}   to the brain. However, it has been found that even people insensitive to a certain smell   {{12}}   can suddenly become sensitive to it when   {{13}}   to it often enough."},{"id":"p-section1-3","text":"The explanation for insensitivity to smell seems to be that the brain finds it   {{14}}   to keep all smell receptors working all the time but can   {{15}}   new receptors if necessary. This may   {{16}}   explain why we are not usually sensitive to our own smells – we simply do not need to be. We are not   {{17}}   of the usual smell of our own house, but we   {{18}}   new smells when we visit someone else's. The brain finds it best to keep smell receptors   {{19}}   for unfamiliar and emergency signals   {{20}}   the smell of smoke, which might indicate the danger of fire."}]},"questions":[{"n":1,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"although"},{"key":"B","text":"as"},{"key":"C","text":"but"},{"key":"D","text":"while"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"1.","reason":"前句是「常被认为不灵敏」，后句给出真相 前半句 Humans are often thought to be insensitive smellers compared with animals 是公认看法，后半句 this is largely because, __(2)__ animals, we stand upright 给出原因——这个原因是要替人嗅觉「翻案」、解释那只是表象。表象 vs 真相，二者关系是转折。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"although 引导让步从句，通常放在分句句首构成「Although X, Y」结构。此处空位于两个独立完整句之间且后接逗号短语，不是让步从句的典型位置；且后半句是要给前半句「找台阶」，不是让自己被前句让步。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"as 在此最可能解作「因为」，那就成了「人嗅觉被认为不灵敏，因为我们直立」——这等于承认人嗅觉真的不灵敏，与下一句 In fact, __(5)__, we are extremely sensitive to smells 的反转立场冲突。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"but 把「人们以为人嗅觉差」与「其实只是因为直立姿势」拧成一个转折，正好接住作者要替人嗅觉翻案的语气，并与第二句 In fact, __(5)__, we are extremely sensitive to smells 形成「转折→反转」的完整链条。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"while 表「然而、与此同时」时要求前后两件事并存对比，如「我喜欢茶，while 他喜欢咖啡」。本句后半句给的是前半句的「成因解释」而非另一件并存事实，逻辑不匹配。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":2,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"above"},{"key":"B","text":"unlike"},{"key":"C","text":"excluding"},{"key":"D","text":"besides"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"2.","reason":"本句强调「人和动物不一样：我们直立」 this is largely because, __(2)__ animals, we stand upright：句子要表达的是「与动物不同，我们是直立的」——「直立」是人独有、与动物相反的特征，所以空处必须传达「不同于动物」的意思。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"above 是物理或抽象「在…之上」，与本句要表达的「与动物不同」无关，硬塞进去意思变成「在动物之上，我们直立」，逻辑不通。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"unlike 直接点出「不同于」，与下文 we stand upright 这一人独有特征严丝合缝；同时呼应全文「人 vs 动物嗅觉对比」的总框架。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"excluding 表把某项从集合中剔除，如「除动物之外」，会把动物从讨论范围里踢出去——可全文恰恰要拿动物做参照对比，方向完全相反。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"besides 引出的是「同类增列」，意思变成「除动物外，我们也直立」——这暗示动物也直立，与事实和文意都冲突。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":3,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"limited"},{"key":"B","text":"committed"},{"key":"C","text":"dedicated"},{"key":"D","text":"confined"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"3.","reason":"对照「漂浮气味 vs 附着气味」的二选一 our noses are __(3)__ to perceiving those smells which float through the air, __(4)__ the majority of smells which stick to surfaces：鼻子只能感知漂浮气味，闻不到大多数附着气味——这是因为人直立而被动「受限」于某一类气味，不是主动「献身」于它。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"limited 的「受限于」中性表达，刚好契合「因直立而只能闻到漂浮气味」这种被动结构性限制，且与下文 __(4)__ the majority of smells which stick to surfaces（闻不到大多数附着气味）形成「能闻这类 vs 不能闻那类」的对照。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"be committed to 多用于人主动献身于事业，如 committed to education。把鼻子说成「献身于闻漂浮气味」明显拟人过头、语体不当。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"dedicated to 与 committed to 同义，都是主动专心于某事，常带敬意色彩。鼻子是被生理限制，并非「专心献身」于漂浮气味。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"confined to 常用于物理空间或病榻（confined to bed），用于「鼻子被禁锢在闻漂浮气味」语义过重、画面别扭；而且本题强调「能力范围」，limited 是最贴当的中性表达。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":4,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"catching"},{"key":"B","text":"ignoring"},{"key":"C","text":"missing"},{"key":"D","text":"tracking"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"4.","reason":"前半「感知漂浮气味」与后半构成对比 our noses are __(3)__ to perceiving those smells which float through the air, __(4)__ the majority of smells which stick to surfaces：前半已说能感知漂浮气味，后半接的就该是「漏掉、闻不到」附着在物体表面的大多数气味——这种「未能闻到」是被动后果。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"如果填 catching，就成了「鼻子也捕捉到了大多数附着气味」，与前半句「受限于漂浮气味」逻辑冲突。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"ignoring 暗示鼻子主动选择不去闻，但事实是因生理结构而闻不到，不是「故意忽略」，主观色彩不符。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"missing 把「能感知漂浮气味，却错过了大部分附着气味」这一被动客观结果讲得最自然，与第 3 题 limited 形成「能力受限 → 错过另一类」的完整因果链。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"tracking 多用于动物追踪猎物气味，强调持续主动跟随。本句讲的是「闻不到」的被动结果，方向不一致。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":5,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"anyway"},{"key":"B","text":"though"},{"key":"C","text":"instead"},{"key":"D","text":"therefore"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"5.","reason":"前面在说人嗅觉「不灵」，本句突然说「极其灵敏」 前文铺垫 our noses are __(3)__ to perceiving those smells which float through the air, __(4)__ the majority of smells which stick to surfaces——给人「人嗅觉受限」的印象，而本句 In fact, __(5)__, we are extremely sensitive to smells 突然抛出反转「我们对气味极其敏感」。In fact + 反转，需要补一个表「不过、然而」的标记。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"anyway 更多是「总之、随便了」的语气，回避前文而非反驳前文，无法承担「In fact，但是」这一反差信号。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"though 用作副词时即「然而、不过」，与 In fact 联手构成「事实上，然而，我们对气味极其灵敏」，把前文「鼻子受限」与本句「极敏感」的对立强调到位。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"instead 要有「不是 A，而是 B」的明确替换结构。本句前文并没有提出某种被替代的方案，仅是观感与事实的对立，不构成 instead 的使用场景。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"therefore 引出「因此」结论，但前文「人嗅觉受限」并不能推出「人嗅觉极敏感」，因果反向，逻辑不通。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":6,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"even if"},{"key":"B","text":"if only"},{"key":"C","text":"only if"},{"key":"D","text":"as if"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"6.","reason":"本句两半是「极敏感」vs「没察觉到」的让步 we are extremely sensitive to smells, __(6)__ we do not generally realize it：前半说极敏感是事实，后半说「我们一般察觉不到」——「察觉不到」并不影响「极敏感」这一事实，因此前半句在「即使后半成立」的情况下仍然成立。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"even if 表「即使」，刚好对应「我们极敏感是事实，即使我们自己一般察觉不到」。这是本句的核心张力。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"if only 用于表达强烈愿望「要是…就好了」，本句没有任何「希望/惋惜」的语气基础。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"only if 意思是「只有当 B 成立，A 才成立」。此处若选它则成「只有当我们意识不到，我们才极敏感」，逻辑荒谬。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"as if 引导「好像…似的」，后接虚拟句或现实句修饰前面的动作。本句前后皆为陈述事实，无比喻关系。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":7,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"distinguishing"},{"key":"B","text":"discovering"},{"key":"C","text":"determining"},{"key":"D","text":"detecting"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"7.","reason":"「even when 浓度被稀释到百万分之一以下」是关键线索 Our noses are capable of __(7)__ human smells even when these are __(8)__ to far below one part in one million：极低浓度仍能被闻到，这正是 detect（探测出极微小信号）的典型场景。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"distinguish 通常 distinguish A from B 或 distinguish between A and B，需要被区分的两方。本句只有「闻到人体气味」一项，无对照对象。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"discover 强调「以前没被人知道，现在被发现」，鼻子日常闻到人体气味并不是首次发现，搭配不当。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"determine 多用于「determine the cause/result/value」等，强调通过分析判定具体结果，本句要的不是「判定」，而是单纯地「闻到」。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"detecting 与「极低浓度也能感知到」高度匹配；这一搭配在科技文体中极常见——烟雾探测、雷达探测都用它。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":8,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"diluted"},{"key":"B","text":"dissolved"},{"key":"C","text":"dispersed"},{"key":"D","text":"diffused"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"8.","reason":"「降到百万分之一以下」是稀释的标准表达 these are __(8)__ to far below one part in one million：one part in one million 是化学/生物里典型的「浓度比」表述，dilute（稀释）的固定搭配就是 dilute X to N parts per million。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"diluted 表「稀释」，与 to far below one part in one million（稀释到远低于百万分之一）形成完美的浓度搭配，且与第 7 空 detect 形成「即使稀释到极低也能探测」的因果。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"dissolve 多搭配 in water/acid 之类的介词短语，表示某物溶解在溶剂里，不与 to + 浓度搭配。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"disperse 多用于人群、烟雾向四面散开，搭配的是空间，而不是浓度比。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"diffuse 指气味在空气中均匀扩散，描述的是扩散过程而非浓度被降低到某个具体值，与 to far below one part in one million 不搭。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":9,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"when"},{"key":"B","text":"since"},{"key":"C","text":"for"},{"key":"D","text":"whereas"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"9.","reason":"前后讲的是两类人对气味的不同反应 some people find that they can smell one type of flower but not another, __(9)__ others are sensitive to the smells of both flowers：前句说「一些人能闻一种花、闻不到另一种」，后句说「另一些人两种花都能闻到」——典型「A vs B」对比。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"when 引导时间状语，需要前后有时间先后或同时关系，而两类人感觉气味本身没有时间序，不通。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"since 当连词表「因为」或「自从」，二者都不与本句的「两类人对照」相符——后句不是前句的原因，也不是起点。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"for 当连词作「因为」（多见于书面语），引出对前句的补充原因。然而后句「另一些人两种花都闻得到」并不能解释「为什么有些人只能闻一种」，反而构成对照。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"whereas 即「然而、相反」，正是「some people... ___ others...」对照句架的标志连词，与本句两类人对气味的不同表现严丝合缝。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":10,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"unusual"},{"key":"B","text":"particular"},{"key":"C","text":"unique"},{"key":"D","text":"typical"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"10.","reason":"上文「闻得到某种花、闻不到另一种」暗示存在「特定」受体 some people find that they can smell one type of flower but not another...This may be because some people do not have the genes necessary to generate __(10)__ smell receptors：能闻 A 种花而闻不到 B 种花，说明每种花对应的是「特定的」受体——缺了对应基因，就少了对应受体。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"unusual 强调「罕见/异常」，本句并不是说这些受体罕见，而是说它们针对特定气味，方向偏。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"particular 强调「具体某一类」，与上文「闻一种花不闻另一种」自然衔接：缺了对应的「特定受体」基因，就少了那一类受体。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"unique 暗示「全世界只有这一种」，超出文意所需的「特定」程度；且后文未做「独一无二」的强调。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"typical 强调「具有代表性的、一般水平的」，本句强调的是「针对某种特定气味」的受体，并非「典型受体」。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":11,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"signs"},{"key":"B","text":"stimuli"},{"key":"C","text":"messages"},{"key":"D","text":"impulses"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"11.","reason":"「细胞感知气味然后把…送到大脑」是关键搭配链 These receptors are the cells which sense smells and send __(11)__ to the brain：sense smells 后接 send ___ to the brain，要选与 send to the brain 搭配最自然、最广义的词——messages。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"sign 偏向「外显的可见迹象」，不直接表示神经传导的信号，且 send signs to the brain 不是惯用搭配。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"stimuli 是大脑/受体所接受的「外部刺激」，本身是被感知的对象，不是受体「发送」出去的东西。方向反了。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"messages 与 send to the brain 完美搭配，准确表达「受体把感觉信号传递给大脑」，是教材级表达。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"impulse 虽指神经冲动，看似贴近，但常说 nerve impulses travel/are transmitted to the brain；与 send 的固定搭配并不如 messages 稳定，且本文行文偏通俗，messages 更合调。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":12,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"at first"},{"key":"B","text":"at all"},{"key":"C","text":"at large"},{"key":"D","text":"at times"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"12.","reason":"「最初闻不到 → 接触够多就能闻到」的转变 even people insensitive to a certain smell __(12)__ can suddenly become sensitive to it when __(13)__ to it often enough：前半「闻不到」、后半「突然能闻到」，二者是时间先后的对比，需要一个表「最初/起初」的短语锁住前半的「最初状态」。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"at first 与下半句「突然变得敏感」构成「起初闻不到 → 后来能闻到」的时间对比，逻辑顺畅。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"at all 多见于 not...at all（完全不）或 if...at all，本句结构上虽含 insensitive，但若填 at all 就成「完全闻不到」的语气强化——与后文「突然能闻到」的变化不冲突但语义重点偏移，且不照应「最初」这一时间起点。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"at large 用于「the public at large」（公众整体）或「criminal at large」（在逃罪犯），与本句逻辑无关。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"at times 表「不时、有时候」，强调间断性，但本句要的是「起初一以贯之地闻不到 → 后来变敏感」的时间转折，而非时不时闻不到。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":13,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"subjected"},{"key":"B","text":"left"},{"key":"C","text":"drawn"},{"key":"D","text":"exposed"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"13.","reason":"「接触次数够多」是 exposed to 的典型场景 can suddenly become sensitive to it when __(13)__ to it often enough：「足够频繁地接触」是 expose 的标准语境（即「频繁暴露」），且后接 often enough 强调累积接触量。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"subjected to 多指「遭受、被强加」如 subjected to torture/criticism，带有不情愿、负面色彩。闻花香一般没有这种被迫感。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"left to 通常接 do sth 或 oneself，意为「留给某人去…」，与「接触某气味」无关。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"drawn to 用于人「被某物吸引」，含主观情感倾向，本句讲的是客观地接触气味，色彩不符。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"exposed 与 to + 气味 + often enough 高度匹配，是「反复接触某物」的最自然表达，且与本段「接触多了就敏感」的语义无缝。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":14,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"ineffective"},{"key":"B","text":"incompetent"},{"key":"C","text":"inefficient"},{"key":"D","text":"insufficient"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"14.","reason":"「全部受体一直工作」对比「按需调用新受体」 the brain finds it __(14)__ to keep all smell receptors working all the time but can __(15)__ new receptors if necessary：前半说「让所有受体一直工作」，后半说「按需才启用新受体」——前半被否定的理由是「不划算、太浪费」，这是 inefficient 的典型语境。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"ineffective 强调「效果差」，若选它则成「让所有受体一直工作没效果」——但事实是有效果，只是耗费太大，方向错。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"incompetent 描述人或机构缺乏胜任能力，不用于描述「保持工作」这类做法的属性，搭配不当。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"inefficient 强调资源浪费，正好解释为什么大脑选择「按需」而非「全开」——这是本段机制解释的核心。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"insufficient 指数量不够，与「保持所有受体一直工作」这个「过多」的行为反向。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":15,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"introduce"},{"key":"B","text":"summon"},{"key":"C","text":"trigger"},{"key":"D","text":"create"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"15.","reason":"「if necessary」点明是按需新增 but can __(15)__ new receptors if necessary：if necessary 表明「需要时才」，且 new receptors 强调是新建出来的，create new receptors 与生物学常见的「神经可塑性、按需生成新受体」对应。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"introduce 强调把已存在的事物带入某场景，与「按需创建新受体」的「从无到有」语义不合。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"summon 多用于召唤人/勇气/记忆等抽象对象（summon courage），用在「细胞受体」上拟人过头。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"trigger new receptors 在生理学上不是常规说法；trigger 多接事件/反应，强调按下开关。本句的核心动作是「让新受体出现」，create 更精确。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"create 与 new receptors 的「从无到有」属性完美匹配，与 if necessary 也一致——按需才创建。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":16,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"still"},{"key":"B","text":"also"},{"key":"C","text":"otherwise"},{"key":"D","text":"nevertheless"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"16.","reason":"前一句解释「按需启用」，本句用同一机制解释「闻不到自家味道」 the brain finds it __(14)__ to keep all smell receptors working all the time but can __(15)__ new receptors if necessary. This may __(16)__ explain why we are not usually sensitive to our own smells：前一句解释为什么大脑按需启用受体，本句用同一原理「也」可以解释「我们对自家气味不敏感」——典型同向补充。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"still 常用作「尽管…但仍…」，需要先有一个让步前提。本句没有让步前提，仅是同一原理的延展应用。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"also 直接表明「这（同一机制）也能解释另一个现象」，与文中以同一原理覆盖多个现象的语篇模式完美吻合。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"otherwise 引出与前句相反假设的结果，本句并未做反向假设，逻辑不通。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"nevertheless 强调「尽管前因如此，结论却相反」，但此处前后是同向延展，没有转折成分。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":17,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"sure"},{"key":"B","text":"sick"},{"key":"C","text":"aware"},{"key":"D","text":"tired"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"17.","reason":"与下半句 notice new smells 形成对比 We are not __(17)__ of the usual smell of our own house, but we __(18)__ new smells when we visit someone else's：前半句要表达「闻不到自家熟悉气味」即「没意识到」，后半句 notice 新气味——前后须形成「察觉↔不察觉」的对比。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"be sure of 表「对…确信」，与「察觉气味」无关；语义跑题。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"be sick of 表「对…感到厌烦」，本句没有厌烦情绪，搭配不对。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"aware 与 of 搭配表「意识到」，是「闻不到自家熟悉气味」的最贴表达，与后半句 notice 完美对照。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"be tired of 与 sick of 类似，表「厌烦、厌倦」，与「察觉气味」无关。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":18,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"tolerate"},{"key":"B","text":"repel"},{"key":"C","text":"neglect"},{"key":"D","text":"notice"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"18.","reason":"与第 17 空 aware 构成反义对照 We are not __(17)__ of the usual smell of our own house, but we __(18)__ new smells when we visit someone else's：前半「察觉不到」，后半要的就是「察觉到」，notice 是「意识到」的标准对译。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"tolerate 表「容忍」，前提是该气味令人不快，本句没有这种语气基础。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"repel 多用于驱赶昆虫、排斥某种刺激，与「察觉到新气味」语义不沾。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"neglect 表主动忽视，正好是 notice 的反面，与「去别人家会闻到新气味」的语义相反。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"notice 是「察觉、注意到」的最标准动词，与第 17 空 aware 形成「不察觉↔察觉」的清晰对比。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":19,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"available"},{"key":"B","text":"reliable"},{"key":"C","text":"identifiable"},{"key":"D","text":"suitable"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"19.","reason":"下文「为陌生与紧急信号留用」点明 available 之义 The brain finds it best to keep smell receptors __(19)__ for unfamiliar and emergency signals __(20)__ the smell of smoke：「为陌生信号和紧急信号留用」要的就是「保持可用/待命」，available 完美对应。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"available 与 keep ___ for 的固定搭配契合，且语义上准确传达「让受体随时可用以应对陌生/紧急信号」。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"reliable 强调「可信赖、不出错」，与「为某用途待命」不是同一维度。受体可靠与否不是本句的重点。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"identifiable 描述「可被辨认出来」，多形容罪犯、物体等，与「保持待命」无关。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"suitable for 多指「适合某用途」，但语义重在「匹配度」而非「随时可调」；本句强调的是「保留以备紧急」，available 更精准。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":20,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"similar to"},{"key":"B","text":"such as"},{"key":"C","text":"along with"},{"key":"D","text":"aside from"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"20.","reason":"「紧急信号 + ___ + 烟味」典型举例结构 unfamiliar and emergency signals __(20)__ the smell of smoke, which might indicate the danger of fire：前面是「陌生与紧急信号」这一类别，后面 the smell of smoke 是其中一个例子——典型类别 + such as + 例子结构。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"similar to 表「与…相似」，需要两个并列对象互相比较；本句烟味是紧急信号的例子，而非与紧急信号相似的另一物。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"such as 是英语正式语体里举例的首选，与「emergency signals, such as the smell of smoke」无缝衔接，并接定语从句进一步说明烟味暗示火灾危险。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"along with 引出并列附加项，本句烟味不是与紧急信号并列的另一类，而是其下属的具体例子，逻辑层级不对。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"aside from 用法接近 besides，表示「除某物之外（还）」，与「举一例」的关系完全不同。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-1","kind":"reading","title":"Text 1","textNo":1,"directions":"Directions: Read the following four texts. Answer the questions after each text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET . (40 points)","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 1","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-1","text":"Everybody loves a fat pay rise. Yet pleasure at your own can vanish if you learn that a colleague has been given a bigger one. Indeed, if he has a reputation for slacking, you might even be outraged. Such behaviour is regarded as \"all too human\", with the underlying assumption that other animals would not be capable of this finely developed sense of grievance. But a study by Sarah Brosnan and Frans de Waal of Emory University in Atlanta, Georgia, which has just been published in Nature, suggests that it is all too monkey, as well."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-2","text":"The researchers studied the behaviour of female brown capuchin monkeys. They look cute. They are good-natured, co-operative creatures, and they share their food readily. Above all, like their female human counterparts, they tend to pay much closer attention to the value of \"goods and services\" than males."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-3","text":"Such characteristics make them perfect candidates for Dr. Brosnan's and Dr. de Waal's study. The researchers spent two years teaching their monkeys to exchange tokens for food. Normally, the monkeys were happy enough to exchange pieces of rock for slices of cucumber. However, when two monkeys were placed in separate but adjoining chambers, so that each could observe what the other was getting in return for its rock, their behaviour became markedly different."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-4","text":"In the world of capuchins grapes are luxury goods (and much preferable to cucumbers). So when one monkey was handed a grape in exchange for her token, the second was reluctant to hand hers over for a mere piece of cucumber. And if one received a grape without having to provide her token in exchange at all, the other either tossed her own token at the researcher or out of the chamber, or refused to accept the slice of cucumber. Indeed, the mere presence of a grape in the other chamber (without an actual monkey to eat it) was enough to induce resentment in a female capuchin."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-5","text":"The researchers suggest that capuchin monkeys, like humans, are guided by social emotions. In the wild, they are a co-operative, group-living species. Such co-operation is likely to be stable only when each animal feels it is not being cheated. Feelings of righteous indignation, it seems, are not the preserve of people alone. Refusing a lesser reward completely makes these feelings abundantly clear to other members of the group. However, whether such a sense of fairness evolved independently in capuchins and humans, or whether it stems from the common ancestor that the species had 35 million years ago, is, as yet, an unanswered question."}]},"questions":[{"n":21,"stem":"In the opening paragraph, the author introduces his topic by____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"posing a contrast."},{"key":"B","text":"justifying an assumption."},{"key":"C","text":"making a comparison."},{"key":"D","text":"explaining a phenomenon."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"在开篇段落中,作者通过____引入主题","choicesZh":{"A":"提出对比","B":"证明一个假设","C":"进行比较","D":"解释一种现象"},"analysis":{"type":"introduces his topic by 问的是开头段用何种手法引出话题，落点是写作手法/段落功能，本质属于段落写作目的的判别，归入例证目的题处理。","parse":"Q21 是一个待补全的不完整陈述句，主干 the author introduces his topic by ____，by 后面接动名词短语表方式；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"Everybody loves a fat pay rise. Yet pleasure at your own can vanish if you learn that a colleague has been given a bigger one. Indeed, if he has a reputation for slacking, you might even be outraged. Such behaviour is regarded as \"all too human\", with the underlying assumption that other animals would not be capable of this finely developed sense of grievance. But a study by Sarah Brosnan and Frans de Waal of Emory University in Atlanta, Georgia, which has just been published in Nature, suggests that it is all too monkey, as well.","locateZh":"人人都喜欢丰厚的加薪。可一旦得知同事拿到的更多，你那点喜悦就会烟消云散。如果这个同事还以偷懒著称，你甚至会勃然大怒。这种行为被认为\"太具人性\"，潜台词是别的动物不会有这种精致的不公感。但亚特兰大埃默里大学的萨拉·布罗斯南和弗朗斯·德瓦尔刚发表于《自然》的一项研究表明，这种感觉对猴子来说\"也太相通\"了。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"posing a contrast 要求两端是相反对立的关系；可原文里人与猴的反应是\"同\"不是\"异\"——都会因不公而愤怒，没有对立面，谈不上 contrast。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文确实提到了一个 underlying assumption（other animals would not be capable of this finely developed sense of grievance），但紧接着的 But 句正是要推翻这个假设，作者根本没在为它辩护，更谈不上 justify（证成）。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"首段从头到尾就在做一件事：把人类对不公的反应与猴子的反应平行起来。这正是 making a comparison 的定义。","pairs":[{"orig":"Such behaviour is regarded as \"all too human\"","opt":"making a comparison"},{"orig":"suggests that it is all too monkey, as well","opt":"making a comparison"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"explaining a phenomenon 要求作者对一个现象作出原因/机制解释，而首段只描述了\"人会愤怒、研究表明猴也会\"两个现象本身，从未解释\"为何会愤怒\"，因此谈不上 explain。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":22,"stem":"The statement \"it is all too monkey\" (Last line, Paragraph 1) implies that____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"monkeys are also outraged by slack rivals."},{"key":"B","text":"resenting unfairness is also monkeys' nature."},{"key":"C","text":"monkeys, like humans, tend to be jealous of each other."},{"key":"D","text":"no animals other than monkeys can develop such emotions."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义句意题","stemZh":"\"it is all too monkey\"这句话(第一段最后一行)暗示____","choicesZh":{"A":"猴子也会对懈怠的竞争对手感到愤怒","B":"憎恨不公平也是猴子的天性","C":"猴子和人类一样,往往会互相嫉妒","D":"除了猴子之外没有其他动物能产生这种情感"},"analysis":{"type":"implies that 要求解释引号那句话暗含的意思，是典型的就句解义。","parse":"Q22 主干 The statement \"it is all too monkey\" implies that ____，主语是带定语的名词短语，谓语 implies 后接 that 引导的宾语从句作补全；结构清晰，无陷阱。","locateEn":"Such behaviour is regarded as \"all too human\", with the underlying assumption that other animals would not be capable of this finely developed sense of grievance. But a study by Sarah Brosnan and Frans de Waal of Emory University in Atlanta, Georgia, which has just been published in Nature, suggests that it is all too monkey, as well.","locateZh":"这种行为被视为\"太具人性\"，潜台词是别的动物不会有这种精致的不公感。但布罗斯南和德瓦尔的研究表明，这种感觉对猴子来说\"也太相通\"了。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"outraged by slack rivals 把首段开头\"同事偷懒\"的人类情景照搬到猴子身上，可原文从未讲过有\"偷懒的猴对手\"——slack rivals 一说在猴的语境里无中生有。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"all too monkey 仿自 all too human，被仿的语义内核是 sense of grievance（不公感），所以\"all too monkey\"=\"猴子也具有这种不公感\"，与 resenting unfairness is also monkeys' nature 等值。","pairs":[{"orig":"this finely developed sense of grievance","opt":"resenting unfairness"},{"orig":"it is all too monkey, as well","opt":"is also monkeys' nature"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"jealous of each other 偷换了 sense of grievance（不公感、被亏待感）的核心——grievance 指对不公正的怨愤，jealous 指对他人所有物的嫉妒；两者不是一回事，原文也无任何\"互相嫉妒\"的语义。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"no animals other than monkeys can develop such emotions 把\"猴有\"夸张成\"只有猴有，其他动物都没\"。原文只用猴子的研究推翻\"别的动物都没有\"这一旧假设，从未排他性断言\"只有猴有\"。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":23,"stem":"Female capuchin monkeys were chosen for the research most probably because they are____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"more inclined to weigh what they get."},{"key":"B","text":"attentive to researchers' instructions."},{"key":"C","text":"nice in both appearance and temperament."},{"key":"D","text":"more generous than their male companions."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"雌性卷尾猴被选作研究对象很可能是因为它们____","choicesZh":{"A":"更倾向于权衡自己得到的东西","B":"留意研究者的指令","C":"在外表和性情上都很好","D":"比雄性同伴更慷慨"},"analysis":{"type":"chosen for the research most probably because 问的是一个具体原因，可直接到原文找对应解释，属典型细节题。","parse":"Q23 主干是被动语态 monkeys were chosen，后接原因状语 because they are ____，从句省略主语 they 已点明指代研究对象；most probably 是程度状语，提醒答案是\"最可能\"而非确凿，但仍以原文为准。","locateEn":"Above all, like their female human counterparts, they tend to pay much closer attention to the value of \"goods and services\" than males.","locateZh":"最重要的是，跟人类的女性对应者一样，雌性卷尾猴倾向于比雄性更密切地关注\"商品与服务\"的价值。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"作者亲自标出 Above all 的特质\"更关注 goods and services 的价值\"，正是 weigh what they get（掂量所获）的同义改写——这就是雌猴入选研究的核心理由。","pairs":[{"orig":"pay much closer attention to the value of \"goods and services\"","opt":"more inclined to weigh what they get"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"attentive to researchers' instructions（注意听研究者指令）在原文中完全没有提及——第 2 段讲的是雌猴对\"价值\"的关注，不是对\"研究者指令\"的关注。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"nice in both appearance and temperament 确实有原文支持（They look cute 与 good-natured），但这两条特征不是\"被选作研究对象\"的关键理由——作者用 Above all 把关键理由单挑出来是\"关注价值\"，外貌温顺只是顺带一提。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文只说雌猴 share their food readily（乐于分享）、pay much closer attention to the value ... than males（在\"关注价值\"上比雄性更甚），并未在\"慷慨\"这个维度上把雌猴和雄性做比较；more generous than their male companions 是把\"分享\"夸成\"更慷慨于雄性\"，越界推断。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":24,"stem":"Dr. Brosnan and Dr. de Waal have eventually found in their study that the monkeys____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"prefer grapes to cucumbers."},{"key":"B","text":"can be taught to exchange things."},{"key":"C","text":"will not be co-operative if feeling cheated."},{"key":"D","text":"are unhappy when separated from others."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"Brosnan博士和de Waal博士最终在研究中发现猴子____","choicesZh":{"A":"喜欢葡萄胜过黄瓜","B":"可以被教会交换东西","C":"如果感觉被欺骗就不会合作","D":"与其他猴子分开时会不高兴"},"analysis":{"type":"eventually found in their study that the monkeys 问研究最终发现了什么，是对原文研究结论的客观转述，属细节题。","parse":"Q24 主干 Dr. Brosnan and Dr. de Waal have eventually found ... that the monkeys ____，have found 是现在完成时表研究已得出结论，that 引导宾语从句作 found 的宾语，从句缺谓语等待选项补全；无陷阱。","locateEn":"The researchers suggest that capuchin monkeys, like humans, are guided by social emotions. In the wild, they are a co-operative, group-living species. Such co-operation is likely to be stable only when each animal feels it is not being cheated.","locateZh":"研究者推断：卷尾猴和人类一样，受社会情感的驱动。在野外，它们是合作的群居物种。这种合作只有在每只动物都觉得自己没有被欺骗时才可能稳定。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"prefer grapes to cucumbers 是第 4 段实验设置的一个事实细节（grapes are luxury goods (and much preferable to cucumbers)），但题干问的是研究的\"最终发现\"——猴子的食物偏好不是研究结论，只是触发不公感的实验素材。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"can be taught to exchange things 取自 The researchers spent two years teaching their monkeys to exchange tokens for food，但这是实验的方法/前置训练，不是研究的最终发现。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"The researchers suggest 给出的核心结论就是\"被骗就闹、就不合作\"，这正是 will not be co-operative if feeling cheated 所表达的内容。","pairs":[{"orig":"Such co-operation is likely to be stable only when each animal feels it is not being cheated","opt":"will not be co-operative if feeling cheated"},{"orig":"Refusing a lesser reward completely makes these feelings abundantly clear to other members of the group","opt":"will not be co-operative"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"are unhappy when separated from others 把 placed in separate but adjoining chambers 这个实验布置（分置两邻室）夸大成\"猴子因被分开而不开心\"——原文从未说猴子因分离而不悦，它们不悦是因为看到同伴拿到葡萄而自己只拿黄瓜。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":25,"stem":"What can we infer from the last paragraph?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Monkeys can be trained to develop social emotions."},{"key":"B","text":"Human indignation evolved from an uncertain source."},{"key":"C","text":"Animals usually show their feelings openly as humans do."},{"key":"D","text":"Cooperation among monkeys remains stable only in the wild."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"推断题","stemZh":"从最后一段我们可以推断出什么?","choicesZh":{"A":"猴子可以被训练出社会情感","B":"人类的愤慨情绪从一个不确定的来源演化而来","C":"动物通常像人类一样公开表达自己的感受","D":"猴子之间的合作只有在野外才保持稳定"},"analysis":{"type":"What can we infer from the last paragraph 同时含 infer 与段落限定，明确属于段内推断题。","parse":"Q25 是一个完整疑问句 What can we infer from the last paragraph?，what 作 infer 的宾语前置，from the last paragraph 为状语限定取证范围；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"However, whether such a sense of fairness evolved independently in capuchins and humans, or whether it stems from the common ancestor that the species had 35 million years ago, is, as yet, an unanswered question.","locateZh":"然而，这种公平感究竟是在卷尾猴和人类身上各自独立演化出来的，还是源自双方在 3500 万年前的共同祖先，至今仍是一个未解之问。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文从未说\"猴子可以被训练出社会情感\"。第 5 段说 capuchin monkeys, like humans, are guided by social emotions——社会情感是它们本来就有的，In the wild, they are a co-operative, group-living species 更说明这是野生天性，非被训练出来的。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"末句明确两种来源皆未定，自然可一步推出\"人的义愤(公平感)源自不确定之处\"，正是 Human indignation evolved from an uncertain source 的意思。","pairs":[{"orig":"such a sense of fairness","opt":"Human indignation"},{"orig":"whether such a sense of fairness evolved independently in capuchins and humans, or whether it stems from the common ancestor that the species had 35 million years ago, is, as yet, an unanswered question","opt":"evolved from an uncertain source"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文 Refusing a lesser reward completely makes these feelings abundantly clear to other members of the group 只是讲卷尾猴用拒收次等奖励的方式表达不满，并未把它推广到\"动物 usually 像人一样公开表达感情\"——usually + animals 是把单一物种行为夸成动物界普遍现象。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Cooperation among monkeys remains stable only in the wild 把原文的合作稳定条件\"未被欺骗\"偷换成了\"地点为野外\"。原文 Such co-operation is likely to be stable only when each animal feels it is not being cheated 中 only when 修饰的是\"未感到被骗\"，不是\"在野外\"。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-2","kind":"reading","title":"Text 2","textNo":2,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 2","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-1","text":"Do you remember all those years when scientists argued that smoking would kill us but the doubters insisted that we didn't know for sure? That the evidence was inconclusive, the science uncertain? That the antismoking lobby was out to destroy our way of life and the government should stay out of the way? Lots of Americans bought that nonsense, and over three decades, some 10 million smokers went to early graves."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-2","text":"There are upsetting parallels today, as scientists in one wave after another try to awaken us to the growing threat of global warming. The latest was a panel from the National Academy of Sciences, enlisted by the White House, to tell us that the Earth's atmosphere is definitely warming and that the problem is largely man-made. The clear message is that we should get moving to protect ourselves. The president of the National Academy, Bruce Alberts, added this key point in the preface to the panel's report: \"Science never has all the answers. But science does provide us with the best available guide to the future, and it is critical that our nation and the world base important policies on the best judgments that science can provide concerning the future consequences of present actions.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-3","text":"Just as on smoking, voices now come from many quarters insisting that the science about global warming is incomplete, that it's OK to keep pouring fumes into the air until we know for sure. This is a dangerous game: by the time 100 percent of the evidence is in, it may be too late. With the risks obvious and growing, a prudent people would take out an insurance policy now."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-4","text":"Fortunately, the White House is starting to pay attention. But it's obvious that a majority of the president's advisers still don't take global warming seriously. Instead of a plan of action, they continue to press for more research – a classic case of \"paralysis by analysis\"."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-5","text":"To serve as responsible stewards of the planet, we must press forward on deeper atmospheric and oceanic research. But research alone is inadequate. If the Administration won't take the legislative initiative, Congress should help to begin fashioning conservation measures. A bill by Democratic Senator Robert Byrd of West Virginia, which would offer financial incentives for private industry, is a promising start. Many see that the country is getting ready to build lots of new power plants to meet our energy needs. If we are ever going to protect the atmosphere, it is crucial that those new plants be environmentally sound."}]},"questions":[{"n":26,"stem":"An argument made by supporters of smoking was that____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"there was no scientific evidence of the correlation between smoking and death."},{"key":"B","text":"the number of early deaths of smokers in the past decades was insignificant."},{"key":"C","text":"people had the freedom to choose their own way of life."},{"key":"D","text":"antismoking people were usually talking nonsense."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"支持吸烟的人提出的一个论点是____","choicesZh":{"A":"吸烟与死亡之间的相关性没有科学证据。","B":"过去几十年中吸烟者的早期死亡人数微不足道。","C":"人们有选择自己生活方式的自由。","D":"反对吸烟的人通常是在胡说八道。"},"analysis":{"type":"An argument made by supporters of smoking was that____ 是补全式陈述句，要求把支持吸烟者的具体主张填进去，属于对原文某一具体说法的还原。","parse":"Q26 主干为 An argument was that ____，过去分词短语 made by supporters of smoking 后置修饰 argument；选项接 that 从句补全。无陷阱结构，只需注意\"made by\"指明这条 argument 是\"支持吸烟者\"提出来的、不是反对者提出来的。","locateEn":"That the antismoking lobby was out to destroy our way of life and the government should stay out of the way?","locateZh":"（他们还坚持说，）反吸烟阵营是要毁掉我们的生活方式，政府就该撒手不管？","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文反方说的是\"证据 inconclusive、science uncertain\"（不充分/不确定），A 项 there was no scientific evidence 直接夸大成\"完全没有科学证据\"，程度被偷改。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文 some 10 million smokers went to early graves 明确说人数惊人；B 项 the number of early deaths of smokers in the past decades was insignificant 把它说成\"微不足道\"，与原文相反。且这是作者陈述，而非支持吸烟者的 argument，立场也错。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判要点\"生活方式自由 + 政府不干预\"在 C 项被合并表述为\"freedom to choose their own way of life\"，正面承接原文反方立场，故锁定 C。","pairs":[{"orig":"the antismoking lobby was out to destroy our way of life","opt":"people had the freedom to choose their own way of life"},{"orig":"the government should stay out of the way","opt":"people had the freedom to choose their own way of life"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文 Lots of Americans bought that nonsense 中 nonsense 是作者对反方说辞的贬称；D 项 antismoking people were usually talking nonsense 反过来把它说成支持吸烟者对反吸烟者的 argument，立场倒置。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":27,"stem":"According to Bruce Alberts, science can serve as____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"a protector."},{"key":"B","text":"a judge."},{"key":"C","text":"a critic."},{"key":"D","text":"a guide."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据Bruce Alberts的说法,科学可以充当____","choicesZh":{"A":"保护者。","B":"法官。","C":"批评者。","D":"向导。"},"analysis":{"type":"According to Bruce Alberts, science can serve as____ 是\"According to 某人，X 是 Y\"的标准细节题题干，答案在原文人名附近的原话里。","parse":"Q27 主干 science can serve as ____，状语 According to Bruce Alberts 限定信息来源；can 表示\"能够、可以\"。结构简单，无理解陷阱。","locateEn":"But science does provide us with the best available guide to the future, and it is critical that our nation and the world base important policies on the best judgments that science can provide concerning the future consequences of present actions.","locateZh":"但科学确实能为我们提供面向未来的最佳可用指南，至关重要的是，国家与世界要把重要政策建立在科学就当下行动之未来后果所能给出的最佳判断之上。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Alberts 的话里没有 protect/guard/defend 等\"保护\"语义，a protector 是文中所无的角色定性。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文 the best judgments that science can provide 中 judgments = \"判断（结论）\"，是科学产出的中性名词；选项 a judge 把 judgments 偷换成施事性 judge（法官/裁判者），词义偷换。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Alberts 通篇是肯定科学的指引功能，并无\"科学批评/挑剔\"语义，a critic 凭空捏造。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判\"指南/向导\"，原文 guide 与选项 a guide 中心词原词复现，故锁定 D。","pairs":[{"orig":"best available guide to the future","opt":"a guide"}]}}}}},{"n":28,"stem":"What does the author mean by \"paralysis by analysis\" (Last line, Paragraph 4)?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Endless studies kill action."},{"key":"B","text":"Careful investigation reveals truth."},{"key":"C","text":"Prudent planning hinders progress."},{"key":"D","text":"Extensive research helps decision-making."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义句意题","stemZh":"作者所说的\"分析导致瘫痪\"(第四段最后一行)是什么意思?","choicesZh":{"A":"无休止的研究扼杀行动。","B":"仔细的调查揭示真相。","C":"谨慎的规划阻碍进步。","D":"广泛的研究有助于决策。"},"analysis":{"type":"What does the author mean by 直接问短语含义，是词义句意题的标准提问句式。","parse":"Q28 主干 What does the author mean by X？X = paralysis by analysis，by 表示\"通过/用……（这个说法）\"。考生易把 by 误解成\"被\"——此处不是被动，是\"通过这个表达\"。","locateEn":"Instead of a plan of action, they continue to press for more research – a classic case of \"paralysis by analysis\".","locateZh":"他们不拿出行动方案，而是不断要求更多研究——这是\"以分析致瘫痪\"的典型案例。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判要点\"无休止研究替代/扼杀行动\"在 A 项被简洁表达为 Endless studies kill action，对应严丝合缝，故选 A。","pairs":[{"orig":"they continue to press for more research","opt":"Endless studies kill action"},{"orig":"Instead of a plan of action","opt":"Endless studies kill action"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文段 they continue to press for more research 是讽刺、贬义；Careful investigation reveals truth 是对调研的正面褒扬，立场反转。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文短语主语是 analysis（分析/研究），C 项 Prudent planning hinders progress 把它换成 planning（规划），且\"hinders progress\"将\"行动被瘫痪\"换成\"进步被阻碍\"，偷换概念。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Extensive research helps decision-making 把原文批评的\"无穷尽研究\"洗成\"广泛研究有助决策\"，正面化程度更甚于 B，立场反转。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":29,"stem":"According to the author, what should the Administration do about global warming?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Offer aid to build cleaner power plants."},{"key":"B","text":"Raise public awareness of conservation."},{"key":"C","text":"Press for further scientific research."},{"key":"D","text":"Take some legislative measures."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据作者的观点,政府应该对全球变暖采取什么措施?","choicesZh":{"A":"提供援助以建造更清洁的发电厂。","B":"提高公众的环保意识。","C":"推动进一步的科学研究。","D":"采取一些立法措施。"},"analysis":{"type":"what should the Administration do about global warming 问的是具体行动方案，是对原文建议句的同义还原，不是观点推断。","parse":"Q29 主干 what should the Administration do？状语 According to the author 锁定立场是作者本人、不是政府或科学家；about global warming 限定话题。结构简单，关键是分清作者立场 vs 政府现状。","locateEn":"But it's obvious that a majority of the president's advisers still don't take global warming seriously. Instead of a plan of action, they continue to press for more research – a classic case of \"paralysis by analysis\".","locateZh":"但显然，总统的大多数顾问仍不认真对待全球变暖。他们没有拿出行动方案，反而继续敦促做更多研究——这是“分析致瘫”的经典案例。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文 A bill by Democratic Senator Robert Byrd of West Virginia, which would offer financial incentives for private industry, is a promising start 是作者举\"国会层面已有的好开端\"，且对象是私营产业；A 项 Offer aid to build cleaner power plants 把举例当作对政府的总体建议，且把\"私营产业的财政激励\"窄化为\"建电厂的援助\"。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文 To serve as responsible stewards of the planet, we must press forward on deeper atmospheric and oceanic research 谈研究与立法，未谈\"提升公众意识\"；B 项 Raise public awareness of conservation 是文中所无的建议方向。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文 But research alone is inadequate 和 they continue to press for more research 明确批评\"只搞研究\"；C 项 Press for further scientific research 复述被批评的做法，恰是作者反对的。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判\"主动出台立法/环保措施\"与 D 项措辞高度同义复现（take + legislative + measures），故锁定 D。","pairs":[{"orig":"take the legislative initiative","opt":"Take some legislative measures"}]}}}}},{"n":30,"stem":"The author associates the issue of global warming with that of smoking because____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"they both suffered from the government's negligence."},{"key":"B","text":"a lesson from the latter is applicable to the former."},{"key":"C","text":"the outcome of the latter aggravates the former."},{"key":"D","text":"both of them have turned from bad to worse."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"作者将全球变暖问题与吸烟问题联系起来是因为____","choicesZh":{"A":"两者都受到政府的忽视。","B":"从后者得到的教训适用于前者。","C":"后者的结果加剧了前者。","D":"两者都已经从坏变得更糟。"},"analysis":{"type":"The author associates the issue of global warming with that of smoking because____ 问的是作者全文的写作意图（为何拿吸烟作类比），考查的是作者的整体论证结构，而非某一段细节。","parse":"Q30 主干 The author associates A with B because____。短语 associate A with B = \"把 A 与 B 联系起来\"；that of smoking 中 that 替代 the issue，避免重复。考生易栽在 that 指代上——that = the issue，不是别的。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文 P1 是\"反方坚持\"和\"大众接受\"导致拖延；并未说\"政府忽视\"造成 1000 万早死。且 A 项 they both suffered from the government's negligence 假定两议题都因政府忽视而恶化——吸烟段未提政府忽视，是\"government should stay out of the way\"的反方诉求被作者批评，与\"negligence\"非同一事。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"主线\"以吸烟教训警示变暖\"= 选项 a lesson from the latter is applicable to the former（latter=smoking, former=global warming）；故锁定 B。","pairs":[{"orig":"Just as on smoking, voices now come from many quarters insisting that the science about global warming is incomplete","opt":"a lesson from the latter is applicable to the former"},{"orig":"There are upsetting parallels today","opt":"a lesson from the latter is applicable to the former"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文没有任何句子说\"吸烟的后果加剧了全球变暖\"，the outcome of the latter aggravates the former 是凭空捏造的因果关系。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文 over three decades, some 10 million smokers went to early graves 是已发生的历史教训；当下文里仅说全球变暖在加剧 the growing threat of global warming，并未提吸烟问题\"turned from bad to worse\"。D 项 both of them have turned from bad to worse 把\"恶化趋势\"硬扣到两端。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-3","kind":"reading","title":"Text 3","textNo":3,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 3","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-1","text":"Of all the components of a good night's sleep, dreams seem to be least within our control. In dreams, a window opens into a world where logic is suspended and dead people speak. A century ago, Freud formulated his revolutionary theory that dreams were the disguised shadows of our unconscious desires and fears; by the late 1970s, neurologists had switched to thinking of them as just \"mental noise\" – the random byproducts of the neural-repair work that goes on during sleep. Now researchers suspect that dreams are part of the mind's emotional thermostat, regulating moods while the brain is \"off-line.\" And one leading authority says that these intensely powerful mental events can be not only harnessed but actually brought under conscious control, to help us sleep and feel better. \"It's your dream,\" says Rosalind Cartwright, chair of psychology at Chicago's Medical Center. \"If you don't like it, change it.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-2","text":"Evidence from brain imaging supports this view. The brain is as active during REM (rapid eye movement) sleep – when most vivid dreams occur – as it is when fully awake, says Dr. Eric Nofzinger at the University of Pittsburgh. But not all parts of the brain are equally involved; the limbic system (the \"emotional brain\") is especially active, while the prefrontal cortex (the center of intellect and reasoning) is relatively quiet. \"We wake up from dreams happy or depressed, and those feelings can stay with us all day.\" says Stanford sleep researcher Dr. William Dement."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-3","text":"The link between dreams and emotions shows up among the patients in Cartwright's clinic. Most people seem to have more bad dreams early in the night, progressing toward happier ones before awakening, suggesting that they are working through negative feelings generated during the day. Because our conscious mind is occupied with daily life we don't always think about the emotional significance of the day's events – until, it appears, we begin to dream."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-4","text":"And this process need not be left to the unconscious. Cartwright believes one can exercise conscious control over recurring bad dreams. As soon as you awaken, identify what is upsetting about the dream. Visualize how you would like it to end instead; the next time it occurs, try to wake up just enough to control its course. With much practice people can learn to, literally, do it in their sleep."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-5","text":"At the end of the day, there's probably little reason to pay attention to our dreams at all unless they keep us from sleeping or \"we wake up in a panic,\" Cartwright says. Terrorism, economic uncertainties and general feelings of insecurity have increased people's anxiety. Those suffering from persistent nightmares should seek help from a therapist. For the rest of us, the brain has its ways of working through bad feelings. Sleep – or rather dream – on it and you'll feel better in the morning."}]},"questions":[{"n":31,"stem":"Researchers have come to believe that dreams____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"can be modified in their courses."},{"key":"B","text":"are susceptible to emotional changes."},{"key":"C","text":"reflect our innermost desires and fears."},{"key":"D","text":"are a random outcome of neural repairs."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"研究人员已经开始相信梦____","choicesZh":{"A":"可以在其过程中被改变。","B":"容易受到情绪变化的影响。","C":"反映我们内心深处的欲望和恐惧。","D":"是神经修复的随机结果。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 Researchers have come to believe that dreams____ 是不完整陈述，要求按原文最新观点直接补全，没有 infer/imply 等推断信号。","parse":"Researchers have come to believe that dreams____ 主干是 Researchers have come to believe + that 宾语从句；从句的主语是 dreams，谓语空缺待选项补全。have come to believe 是完成体，强调'最近才开始相信'，暗示与早期看法对比。","locateEn":"Now researchers suspect that dreams are part of the mind's emotional thermostat, regulating moods while the brain is \"off-line.\" And one leading authority says that these intensely powerful mental events can be not only harnessed but actually brought under conscious control, to help us sleep and feel better.","locateZh":"现在研究者怀疑，梦是大脑情绪调温器的一部分，在大脑'离线'时调节情绪。一位权威人士还说，这些强大的心理活动不仅可以被驾驭，甚至可以被纳入有意识的控制，以帮助我们更好地入睡和感受。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判'梦可被有意识控制'，选项 A 的 modified in their courses 正是对 brought under conscious control 与 change it 的上位同义替换，唯一命中。","pairs":[{"orig":"brought under conscious control","opt":"can be modified in their courses"},{"orig":"If you don't like it, change it.","opt":"can be modified in their courses"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文是梦影响情绪（We wake up from dreams happy or depressed），不是情绪改变梦；选项 are susceptible to emotional changes（梦受情绪变化影响）把方向颠倒，且与 Q31 所问的'researchers 新观点'无关。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"reflect our innermost desires and fears 复述的是 Freud formulated his revolutionary theory that dreams were the disguised shadows of our unconscious desires and fears，属于'a century ago'的旧观点，并非 Q31 所问的 researchers 'come to believe'。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"are a random outcome of neural repairs 对应 by the late 1970s, neurologists had switched to thinking of them as just \"mental noise\" – the random byproducts of the neural-repair work that goes on during sleep，是上一代神经学家的旧观点，不是当下 researchers 的看法。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":32,"stem":"By referring to the limbic system, the author intends to show____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"its function in our dreams."},{"key":"B","text":"the mechanism of REM sleep."},{"key":"C","text":"the relation of dreams to emotions."},{"key":"D","text":"its difference from the prefrontal cortex."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"通过提及边缘系统,作者意图表明____","choicesZh":{"A":"它在我们梦中的功能。","B":"快速眼动睡眠的机制。","C":"梦与情绪的关系。","D":"它与前额叶皮层的区别。"},"analysis":{"type":"By referring to the limbic system, the author intends to show____ 是典型'借由提及 X，作者要说明 Y'的例证目的句式。","parse":"By referring to the limbic system, the author intends to show____ 句首 By + 动名词短语作方式状语，主句 the author intends to show + 宾语从句留空。注意 intends to show 的宾语不是 limbic system，而是空格处要补的'论点'，考生最易把 limbic system 的定义当答案。","locateEn":"Evidence from brain imaging supports this view. The brain is as active during REM (rapid eye movement) sleep – when most vivid dreams occur – as it is when fully awake, says Dr. Eric Nofzinger at the University of Pittsburgh. But not all parts of the brain are equally involved; the limbic system (the \"emotional brain\") is especially active, while the prefrontal cortex (the center of intellect and reasoning) is relatively quiet.","locateZh":"脑成像证据支持这种观点。匹兹堡大学的 Eric Nofzinger 博士说，REM 睡眠（最生动的梦发生时）期间，大脑活跃程度与完全清醒时相当。但并非大脑各部分都同等参与；'情绪脑'limbic system 尤其活跃，而'理性中枢'前额叶则相对安静。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"its function in our dreams 把焦点放在 limbic system 在梦中的功能本身，可作者意图是借它论证'梦—情绪'关系（更上一层的论点），不是介绍这个脑区的功能。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"the mechanism of REM sleep（REM 睡眠的机制）借用了段中 REM (rapid eye movement) sleep 的眼熟词；但作者并未解释 REM 是如何运作的，只是把它作为'梦发生的时段'背景提及。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"段首 supports this view + 段中 'emotional brain' 同位语 + 段末 happy or depressed 三处合力，证明引 limbic system 就是为了显示梦与情绪挂钩，C 命中。","pairs":[{"orig":"Evidence from brain imaging supports this view.","opt":"the relation of dreams to emotions"},{"orig":"the limbic system (the \"emotional brain\") is especially active","opt":"the relation of dreams to emotions"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"its difference from the prefrontal cortex 抓住的是 limbic 与 prefrontal cortex 的对比这一手段，但作者引这个对比仍是为了凸显'情绪脑活跃—梦关情绪'，不是为对比而对比。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":33,"stem":"The negative feelings generated during the day tend to____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"aggravate in our unconscious mind."},{"key":"B","text":"develop into happy dreams."},{"key":"C","text":"persist till the time we fall asleep."},{"key":"D","text":"show up in dreams early at night."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"白天产生的负面情绪往往会____","choicesZh":{"A":"在我们的无意识思维中加剧。","B":"发展成愉快的梦。","C":"持续到我们入睡的时候。","D":"在夜间早些时候出现在梦中。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The negative feelings generated during the day tend to____ 直接陈述待补全，问'倾向于发生什么'，是事实描述。","parse":"The negative feelings generated during the day tend to____ 主干是 The negative feelings tend to + 不定式空缺；过去分词短语 generated during the day 后置修饰 feelings，意为'白天产生的负面情绪'。","locateEn":"Most people seem to have more bad dreams early in the night, progressing toward happier ones before awakening, suggesting that they are working through negative feelings generated during the day.","locateZh":"多数人前半夜噩梦较多，临醒前逐渐转向更愉快的梦境，这说明他们在'消化'白天产生的负面情绪。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"aggravate in our unconscious mind（在无意识中恶化）原文从未提；并且原文说大脑通过梦'消化/处理'负面情绪（working through = 解决、走完），趋势是缓解，不是恶化。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文是'梦境的整体走向'从噩梦逐渐变成愉快梦境（progressing toward happier ones before awakening），主语是 bad dreams，不是 negative feelings；选项 develop into happy dreams 把'梦发展为愉快梦'移花接木到'负面情绪发展为愉快梦'，主语错位。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"persist till the time we fall asleep（持续到入睡）原文没说'白天负面情绪要撑到入睡才消'，相反第三段说我们白天'忙着 daily life' 顾不上情绪意义，到了梦里才处理（we don't always think about the emotional significance of the day's events – until ... we begin to dream），但这并不等于情绪本身'持续到入睡'。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"'消化负面情绪'与'负面情绪显现于梦中'是同一过程的两端表述，D 直接说'前半夜在梦里现身'，正命中预判。","pairs":[{"orig":"Most people seem to have more bad dreams early in the night ... they are working through negative feelings generated during the day","opt":"show up in dreams early at night"},{"orig":"more bad dreams early in the night","opt":"early at night"}]}}}}},{"n":34,"stem":"Cartwright seems to suggest that____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"waking up in time is essential to the ridding of bad dreams."},{"key":"B","text":"visualizing bad dreams helps bring them under control."},{"key":"C","text":"dreams should be left to their natural progression."},{"key":"D","text":"dreaming may not entirely belong to the unconscious."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"推断题","stemZh":"Cartwright似乎暗示____","choicesZh":{"A":"及时醒来对于摆脱噩梦至关重要。","B":"想象噩梦有助于将它们控制住。","C":"应该让梦自然发展。","D":"做梦可能并不完全属于无意识。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 Cartwright seems to suggest that____ 含 seems to suggest，问的是言外之意而非原文字面表述。","parse":"Cartwright seems to suggest that____ 主干 Cartwright + 系动词性短语 seems to + suggest + that 从句空缺。seems 这个软化词不影响理解，重点是 suggest 提示'隐含'。","locateEn":"And this process need not be left to the unconscious. Cartwright believes one can exercise conscious control over recurring bad dreams.","locateZh":"而这个过程不必交给无意识来完成。Cartwright 认为，人可以对反复出现的噩梦施加有意识的控制。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文是说'醒后 + 想象新结局 + 下次梦中刚好醒一点点以掌控走向'三步配合（As soon as you awaken, identify what is upsetting ... try to wake up just enough to control its course），并未说'及时醒来本身就是摆脱噩梦的关键'。选项 waking up in time is essential to the ridding of bad dreams 单把'及时醒来'拎出来当 essential 条件，属于过度推断。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文 Visualize how you would like it to end instead 让 visualize 的是'想要的结局'，不是'噩梦本身'；选项 visualizing bad dreams helps bring them under control 把可视化对象从'新结局'偷换成'噩梦'，宾语错位。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文核心主张正是'梦不必交给无意识、可主动干预'，选项 dreams should be left to their natural progression（应让梦自然发展）与 Cartwright 立场完全相反。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判'梦不完全是无意识'，选项 D 用'may not entirely belong to the unconscious'恰好同向重写，且 may / entirely 控制好了推断强度，没越界——命中。","pairs":[{"orig":"And this process need not be left to the unconscious.","opt":"dreaming may not entirely belong to the unconscious"},{"orig":"Cartwright believes one can exercise conscious control over recurring bad dreams.","opt":"dreaming may not entirely belong to the unconscious"}]}}}}},{"n":35,"stem":"What advice might Cartwright give to those who sometimes have bad dreams?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Lead your life as usual."},{"key":"B","text":"Seek professional help."},{"key":"C","text":"Exercise conscious control."},{"key":"D","text":"Avoid anxiety in the daytime."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"Cartwright可能会给那些有时做噩梦的人什么建议?","choicesZh":{"A":"像往常一样生活。","B":"寻求专业帮助。","C":"进行有意识的控制。","D":"避免白天的焦虑。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 What advice might Cartwright give to those who sometimes have bad dreams? 直接问'对偶尔做噩梦者会给什么建议'，是事实细节复述。","parse":"What advice might Cartwright give to those who sometimes have bad dreams? 主干 What advice + might Cartwright give；介宾结构 to those + 定语从句 who sometimes have bad dreams 修饰 those。整句问'会对一类人提什么建议'，sometimes 必须读准——是偶发而非长期。","locateEn":"At the end of the day, there's probably little reason to pay attention to our dreams at all unless they keep us from sleeping or \"we wake up in a panic,\" Cartwright says. ... Those suffering from persistent nightmares should seek help from a therapist. For the rest of us, the brain has its ways of working through bad feelings. Sleep – or rather dream – on it and you'll feel better in the morning.","locateZh":"Cartwright 说，归根到底，除非噩梦影响睡眠或让你惊醒，否则几乎没必要在意我们的梦……持续做噩梦的人应该去找治疗师；对我们其他人，大脑自有办法消化坏情绪——睡一觉（或者说梦一觉），早上就好了。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"题干限定 sometimes have bad dreams 属于 the rest of us 而非 persistent nightmares 人群，对应建议是不必当回事、照常生活，A 命中。","pairs":[{"orig":"there's probably little reason to pay attention to our dreams at all","opt":"Lead your life as usual."},{"orig":"For the rest of us, the brain has its ways of working through bad feelings.","opt":"Lead your life as usual."}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"Seek professional help 复述的是 Those suffering from persistent nightmares should seek help from a therapist——那是给'持续噩梦'人群的建议，不是给题干所问的'偶尔做噩梦'人群。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Exercise conscious control. 复述的是 Cartwright believes one can exercise conscious control over recurring bad dreams——那是讲 recurring（反复出现的）噩梦该如何主动干预，不是给'偶尔做噩梦者'的建议。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文 Terrorism, economic uncertainties and general feelings of insecurity have increased people's anxiety 只是解释为何当下人们焦虑加剧，从未建议'白天避免焦虑'；Cartwright 也没有以'避开 anxiety'作为对偶尔做噩梦者的处方。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-4","kind":"reading","title":"Text 4","textNo":4,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 4","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-1","text":"Americans no longer expect public figures, whether in speech or in writing, to command the English language with skill and gift. Nor do they aspire to such command themselves. In his latest book, Doing Our Own Thing: The Degradation of Language and Music and Why We Should Like, Care, John McWhorter, a linguist and controversialist of mixed liberal and conservative views, sees the triumph of 1960s counter-culture as responsible for the decline of formal English."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-2","text":"Blaming the permissive 1960s is nothing new, but this is not yet another criticism against the decline in education. Mr. McWhorter's academic speciality is language history and change, and he sees the gradual disappearance of \"whom\", for example, to be natural and no more regrettable than the loss of the case-endings of Old English."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-3","text":"But the cult of the authentic and the personal, \"doing our own thing\", has spelt the death of formal speech, writing, poetry and music. While even the modestly educated sought an elevated tone when they put pen to paper before the 1960s, even the most well regarded writing since then has sought to capture spoken English on the page. Equally, in poetry, the highly personal, performative genre is the only form that could claim real liveliness. In both oral and written English, talking is triumphing over speaking, spontaneity over craft."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-4","text":"Illustrated with an entertaining array of examples from both high and low culture, the trend that Mr. McWhorter documents is unmistakable. But it is less clear, to take the question of his subtitle, why we should, like, care. As a linguist, he acknowledges that all varieties of human language, including non-standard ones like Black English, can be powerfully expressive – there exists no language or dialect in the world that cannot convey complex ideas. He is not arguing, as many do, that we can no longer think straight because we do not talk proper."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-5","text":"Russians have a deep love for their own language and carry large chunks of memorized poetry in their heads, while Italian politicians tend to elaborate speech that would seem old-fashioned to most English-speakers. Mr. McWhorter acknowledges that formal language is not strictly necessary, and proposes no radical education reforms – he is really grieving over the loss of something beautiful more than useful. We now take our English \"on paper plates instead of china\". A shame, perhaps, but probably an inevitable one."}]},"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"According to Mc Whorter, the decline of formal English____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"is inevitable in radical education reforms."},{"key":"B","text":"is but all too natural in language development."},{"key":"C","text":"has caused the controversy over the counter-culture."},{"key":"D","text":"brought about changes in public attitudes in the 1960s."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据McWhorter的观点,正式英语的衰落____","choicesZh":{"A":"在激进的教育改革中是不可避免的。","B":"在语言发展中是再自然不过的。","C":"引起了关于反主流文化的争议。","D":"在20世纪60年代带来了公众态度的变化。"},"analysis":{"type":"According to Mc Whorter, the decline of formal English____ 是典型 \"According to + 人/书\" 句式，要求复述原文某一具体观点。","parse":"Q36 为待补全陈述句，主干是 \"the decline of formal English ___\"，选项是谓语延伸；\"According to Mc Whorter\" 为状语，限定观点归属——务必抓住是 Mc Whorter 的看法，不是作者本人立场。","locateEn":"Mr. McWhorter's academic speciality is language history and change, and he sees the gradual disappearance of \"whom\", for example, to be natural and no more regrettable than the loss of the case-endings of Old English.","locateZh":"麦克沃特的学术专长是语言史和语言演变，他举例说\"whom\"的逐渐消失是自然的，并不比古英语格尾的消失更可惜。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"is inevitable in radical education reforms 说衰落是激进教改的必然结果，原文 proposes no radical education reforms 反而明示麦克沃特不提任何激进教改，根本未把 decline 与 reforms 挂钩。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判的\"自然演变\"在 but all too natural in language development 里被 natural+language development 精确对应，无任何添加。","pairs":[{"orig":"natural and no more regrettable than the loss of the case-endings of Old English","opt":"but all too natural in language development"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"has caused the controversy over the counter-culture 说衰落造成了对反主流文化的争议；原文 sees the triumph of 1960s counter-culture as responsible for the decline of formal English 恰是反主流文化\"造成\"了衰落，因果反过来。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"brought about changes in public attitudes in the 1960s 说衰落在 1960s 引起了公众态度变化；原文恰是 1960s 反主流文化先发生、形式英语随后衰落，并未说 decline 反过来去改变 1960s 的公众态度。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":37,"stem":"The word \"talking\" (Line 6, Paragraph 3) denotes____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"modesty."},{"key":"B","text":"personality."},{"key":"C","text":"liveliness."},{"key":"D","text":"informality."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义句意题","stemZh":"第三段第六行中的\"talking\"一词表示____","choicesZh":{"A":"谦逊。","B":"个性。","C":"生动性。","D":"非正式性。"},"analysis":{"type":"The word \"talking\" (Line 6, Paragraph 3) denotes____ 用 denotes 直接问某词在文中所指。","parse":"Q37 主干 \"The word talking denotes ___\"，括号内 Line 6, Paragraph 3 是定位坐标，denotes 是关键谓词——它要的是\"该词在此处对应什么含义\"，不是该词的常规字面义。","locateEn":"In both oral and written English, talking is triumphing over speaking, spontaneity over craft.","locateZh":"无论口头还是书面英语，\"说话式\"都在压倒\"讲究式\"，即兴正在压倒雕琢。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"modesty 取自 the modestly educated 的字面联想，但 modestly 在原文修饰受教育程度，与 talking 的语境义无关。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"personality 来自 the highly personal, performative genre，是本段大主题；但 talking 这一个词的具体内涵是\"不正式\"，并非整段主题词。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"liveliness 出自 could claim real liveliness，是对 personal performative genre 的评价词，描述\"生动\"，并非 talking 的含义。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判的\"不讲究、不正式\"在四个选项中只对应 informality。","pairs":[{"orig":"talking is triumphing over speaking, spontaneity over craft","opt":"informality"}]}}}}},{"n":38,"stem":"To which of the following statements would McWhorter most likely agree?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Logical thinking is not necessarily related to the way we talk."},{"key":"B","text":"Black English can be more expressive than standard English."},{"key":"C","text":"Non-standard varieties of human language are just as entertaining."},{"key":"D","text":"Of all the varieties, standard English can best convey complex ideas."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"推断题","stemZh":"McWhorter最有可能同意以下哪项陈述?","choicesZh":{"A":"逻辑思维不一定与我们说话的方式相关。","B":"黑人英语可以比标准英语更具表现力。","C":"人类语言的非标准变体同样具有娱乐性。","D":"在所有变体中,标准英语最能传达复杂的思想。"},"analysis":{"type":"To which of the following statements would McWhorter most likely agree? 直接是\"他最可能同意\"的虚拟疑问，标准推断题。","parse":"Q38 是 To which ... would McWhorter agree 的倒装疑问句，which 引导的从句作 agree 的宾语；would 是虚拟语气表\"最可能\"，与 most likely 叠加强化\"推断\"信号。","locateEn":"He is not arguing, as many do, that we can no longer think straight because we do not talk proper.","locateZh":"他并不像许多人那样主张：因为我们说话不规范，我们就再也没法清晰思考了。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判的\"思维和说话方式无必然联系\"直接对应 Logical thinking is not necessarily related to the way we talk.。","pairs":[{"orig":"He is not arguing, as many do, that we can no longer think straight because we do not talk proper.","opt":"Logical thinking is not necessarily related to the way we talk."}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"Black English can be more expressive than standard English. 说黑人英语比标准英语\"更具\"表现力；原文 all varieties of human language, including non-standard ones like Black English, can be powerfully expressive 只说所有变体\"都\"富有表现力，没做谁强谁弱的比较。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Non-standard varieties of human language are just as entertaining. 把原文 expressive（富有表现力）偷换为 entertaining（有娱乐性）；entertaining 出现在 Illustrated with an entertaining array of examples，修饰的是举例本身而非语言变体。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Of all the varieties, standard English can best convey complex ideas. 称标准英语最能传达复杂思想；原文 there exists no language or dialect in the world that cannot convey complex ideas 明确说\"世上没有哪种语言或方言不能传达复杂思想\"，即所有变体在这点上等同。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":39,"stem":"The description of Russians' love of memorizing poetry shows the author's____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"interest in their language."},{"key":"B","text":"appreciation of their efforts."},{"key":"C","text":"admiration for their memory."},{"key":"D","text":"contempt for their old-fashionedness."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"态度题","stemZh":"对俄罗斯人热爱背诵诗歌的描述显示了作者的____","choicesZh":{"A":"对他们语言的兴趣。","B":"对他们努力的赞赏。","C":"对他们记忆力的钦佩。","D":"对他们守旧的轻蔑。"},"analysis":{"type":"shows the author's____ 直接索要作者态度词，且选项均为情感/评价名词（interest/appreciation/admiration/contempt）。","parse":"Q39 主干 \"The description ... shows the author's ___\"，主语是\"对俄国人背诗的描写\"这一动作名词化短语，shows 后接情感名词作宾语；注意立场归属是 the author's 而非 Russians 自己的。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"interest in their language 把对象从\"努力\"换成\"俄语本身\"；作者并未表态对俄语这门语言感兴趣，所要肯定的是俄国人\"维护母语\"的做法。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判的\"正面肯定 + 落在努力上\"与 appreciation of their efforts 双匹配——appreciation 给出正向极性，efforts 锁住\"背诵\"这一持续努力的对象。","pairs":[{"orig":"grieving over the loss of something beautiful more than useful","opt":"appreciation of their efforts"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"admiration for their memory 把肯定对象偏向\"记忆力\"这种生理能力；原文重心是\"用心、用情维护母语\"的态度，而非\"记得住\"本身。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"contempt for their old-fashionedness 是负面态度且对象指向\"陈旧\"；原文 old-fashioned 是用在意大利政客身上、且只是 most English-speakers 的看法，作者本人没表态轻蔑，更没用在俄国人身上。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":40,"stem":"According to the last paragraph, \"paper plates\" is to \"china\" as____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"\"temporary\" is to \"permanent\"."},{"key":"B","text":"\"radical\" is to \"conservative\"."},{"key":"C","text":"\"functional\" is to \"artistic\"."},{"key":"D","text":"\"humble\" is to \"noble\"."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义句意题","stemZh":"根据最后一段,\"纸盘\"之于\"瓷器\"正如____","choicesZh":{"A":"\"临时的\"之于\"永久的\"。","B":"\"激进的\"之于\"保守的\"。","C":"\"功能性的\"之于\"艺术性的\"。","D":"\"朴素的\"之于\"高贵的\"。"},"analysis":{"type":"\"paper plates\" is to \"china\" as____ 是 X∶Y∷A∶B 类比题型，本质是问比喻含义。","parse":"Q40 借 \"X is to Y as A is to B\" 类比结构出题，as 引出比例关系；空格处须填一组保持与 paper plates∶china 同一对立关系的对子，注意比对的是\"关系\"而不是单词义。","locateEn":"Mr. McWhorter acknowledges that formal language is not strictly necessary, and proposes no radical education reforms – he is really grieving over the loss of something beautiful more than useful. We now take our English \"on paper plates instead of china\". A shame, perhaps, but probably an inevitable one.","locateZh":"麦克沃特承认正式语言并非严格必要，也不主张任何激进的教育改革——他实际上是在为某种美的、而非实用的东西的消逝而悲伤。如今我们\"用纸盘而非瓷器\"盛着自己的英语。也许遗憾，但大概在所难免。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"\"temporary\" is to \"permanent\" 给的是\"短暂-永久\"时间对立；纸盘虽是一次性，但作者强调的不是\"短暂\"，而是\"将就实用\"，china 也不是因\"永久\"而对照。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"\"radical\" is to \"conservative\" 是\"激进-保守\"政治/态度对立；原文 proposes no radical education reforms 里的 radical 是别处用法，与 paper plates∶china 的器物比喻毫无关联。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"四个选项中只有 \"functional\" is to \"artistic\" 同时满足\"实用-艺术\"对立且方向与 paper plates∶china 一致。","pairs":[{"orig":"grieving over the loss of something beautiful more than useful","opt":"\"functional\" is to \"artistic\""},{"orig":"We now take our English \"on paper plates instead of china\".","opt":"\"functional\" is to \"artistic\""}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"\"humble\" is to \"noble\" 是\"卑微-高贵\"的社会阶级对立；纸盘并不含\"卑微\"的阶级贬义，作者也未把 china 抬到\"高贵\"的阶级位，他强调的是\"美与实用\"，不是\"贵贱\"。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-b","kind":"matching","title":"Section II Part B 选句填空","directions":"Directions: In the following text, some sentences have been removed. For Questions 41-45, choose the most suitable one from the list A-G to fit into each of the numbered blanks. There are two extra choices, which do not fit in any of the gaps. Mark your answers on ANSWER SHEET 1. (10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-b-1","label":"","text":"Canada's premiers (the leaders of provincial governments), if they have any breath left after complaining about Ottawa at their late July annual meeting, might spare a moment to do something, together, to reduce health-care costs."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-2","label":"","text":"They're all groaning about soaring health budgets, the fastest-growing component of which are pharmaceutical costs."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-3","label":"","text":"{{41}}"},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-4","label":"","text":"What to do? Both the Romanow commission and the Kirby committee on health care – to say nothing of reports from other experts – recommended the creation of a national drug agency. Instead of each province having its own list of approved drugs, bureaucracy, procedures and limited bargaining power, all would pool resources, work with Ottawa, and create a national institution."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-5","label":"","text":"{{42}}"},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-6","label":"","text":"But \"national\" doesn't have to mean that. \"National\" could mean interprovincial – provinces combining efforts to create one body."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-7","label":"","text":"Either way, one benefit of a \"national\" organization would be to negotiate better prices, if possible, with drug manufacturers. Instead of having one province – or a series of hospitals within a province – negotiate a price for a given drug on the provincial list, the national agency would negotiate on behalf of all provinces."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-8","label":"","text":"Rather than, say, Quebec, negotiating on behalf of seven million people, the national agency would negotiate on behalf of 31 million people. Basic economics suggests the greater the potential consumers, the higher the likelihood of a better price."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-9","label":"","text":"{{43}}"},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-10","label":"","text":"A small step has been taken in the direction of a national agency with the creation of the Canadian Co-ordinating Office for Health Technology Assessment, funded by Ottawa and the provinces. Under it, a Common Drug Review recommends to provincial lists which new drugs should be included. Predictably, and regrettably, Quebec refused to join."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-11","label":"","text":"A few premiers are suspicious of any federal-provincial deal-making. They (particularly Quebec and Alberta) just want Ottawa to fork over additional billions with few, if any, strings attached. That's one reason why the idea of a national list hasn't gone anywhere, while drug costs keep rising fast."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-12","label":"","text":"{{44}}"},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-13","label":"","text":"Premiers love to quote Mr. Romanow's report selectively, especially the parts about more federal money. Perhaps they should read what he had to say about drugs: \"A national drug agency would provide governments more influence on pharmaceutical companies in order to try to constrain the ever-increasing cost of drugs.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-14","label":"","text":"{{45}}"},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-15","label":"","text":"So when the premiers gather in Niagara Falls to assemble their usual complaint list, they should also get cracking about something in their jurisdiction that would help their budgets and patients."}]},"choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Quebec's resistance to a national agency is provincialist ideology. One of the first advocates for a national list was a researcher at Laval University. Quebec's Drug Insurance Fund has seen its costs skyrocket with annual increases from 14.3 per cent to 26.8 per cent!"},{"key":"B","text":"Or they could read Mr. Kirby's report: \"The substantial buying power of such an agency would strengthen the public prescription-drug insurance plans to negotiate the lowest possible purchase prices from drug companies.\""},{"key":"C","text":"What does \"national\" mean? Roy Romanow and Senator Michael Kirby recommended a federal-provincial body much like the recently created National Health Council."},{"key":"D","text":"The problem is simple and stark: health-care costs have been, are, and will continue to increase faster than government revenues."},{"key":"E","text":"According to the Canadian Institute for Health Information, prescription drug costs have risen since 1997 at twice the rate of overall health-care spending. Part of the increase comes from drugs being used to replace other kinds of treatments. Part of it arises from new drugs costing more than older kinds. Part of it is higher prices."},{"key":"F","text":"So, if the provinces want to run the health-care show, they should prove they can run it, starting with an interprovincial health list that would end duplication, save administrative costs, prevent one province from being played off against another, and bargain for better drug prices."},{"key":"G","text":"Of course, the pharmaceutical companies will scream. They like divided buyers; they can lobby better that way. They can use the threat of removing jobs from one province to another. They can hope that, if one province includes a drug on its list, the pressure will cause others to include it on theirs. They wouldn't like a national agency, but self-interest would lead them to deal with it."}],"questions":[{"n":41,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Quebec's resistance to a national agency is provincialist ideology. One of the first advocates for a national list was a researcher at Laval University. Quebec's Drug Insurance Fund has seen its costs skyrocket with annual increases from 14.3 per cent to 26.8 per cent!"},{"key":"B","text":"Or they could read Mr. Kirby's report: \"The substantial buying power of such an agency would strengthen the public prescription-drug insurance plans to negotiate the lowest possible purchase prices from drug companies.\""},{"key":"C","text":"What does \"national\" mean? Roy Romanow and Senator Michael Kirby recommended a federal-provincial body much like the recently created National Health Council."},{"key":"D","text":"The problem is simple and stark: health-care costs have been, are, and will continue to increase faster than government revenues."},{"key":"E","text":"According to the Canadian Institute for Health Information, prescription drug costs have risen since 1997 at twice the rate of overall health-care spending. Part of the increase comes from drugs being used to replace other kinds of treatments. Part of it arises from new drugs costing more than older kinds. Part of it is higher prices."},{"key":"F","text":"So, if the provinces want to run the health-care show, they should prove they can run it, starting with an interprovincial health list that would end duplication, save administrative costs, prevent one province from being played off against another, and bargain for better drug prices."},{"key":"G","text":"Of course, the pharmaceutical companies will scream. They like divided buyers; they can lobby better that way. They can use the threat of removing jobs from one province to another. They can hope that, if one province includes a drug on its list, the pressure will cause others to include it on theirs. They wouldn't like a national agency, but self-interest would lead them to deal with it."}],"answer":"E","explanation":{"answer":"E","type":"独占整段","stemZh":"41. (41)____","reason":"According to the Canadian Institute for Health Information, prescription drug costs have risen since 1997 at twice the rate of overall health-care spending. Part of the increase comes from drugs being used to replace other kinds of treatments. Part of it arises from new drugs costing more than older kinds. Part of it is higher prices. 根据加拿大卫生信息研究所的数据,自 1997 年以来处方药成本的增长速度是整体医疗保健支出增长速度的两倍。部分增长来自药物被用于替代其他类型的治疗。部分增长源于新药比旧药价格更高。部分增长是价格上涨。\n上句点出药费最快、下段问‘怎么办’ 空格前第 2 段说医疗预算飞涨、增长最快的是药费（the fastest-growing component of which are pharmaceutical costs）；空格后第 4 段以 What to do? 转入对策。夹在中间的这一空，应当是把‘药费为什么这么严重’具体说清的一句，为下文‘怎么办’铺垫。\n复现药费且数据坐实 E 句 prescription drug costs have risen since 1997 at twice the rate 正是对前段‘药费增长最快’的数据化展开，话题严丝合缝；其后‘What to do?’顺理成章。既接得上上文、又接得下下文，双向缝合成立。","options":{},"choiceEn":"According to the Canadian Institute for Health Information, prescription drug costs have risen since 1997 at twice the rate of overall health-care spending. Part of the increase comes from drugs being used to replace other kinds of treatments. Part of it arises from new drugs costing more than older kinds. Part of it is higher prices.","clues":["词汇复现 药费 = pharmaceutical / drug costs 前段的关键词是 pharmaceutical costs，正确句应当再次提到‘药费’这一同义说法（prescription drug costs），并把‘增长最快’落到具体数据上。","逻辑连接 先摆事实再问对策 后段 What to do? 是‘面对问题问对策’，说明这一空必须先把‘问题’陈述完整、且是关于药费的具体问题，逻辑上才接得上‘怎么办’。"]}},{"n":42,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Quebec's resistance to a national agency is provincialist ideology. One of the first advocates for a national list was a researcher at Laval University. Quebec's Drug Insurance Fund has seen its costs skyrocket with annual increases from 14.3 per cent to 26.8 per cent!"},{"key":"B","text":"Or they could read Mr. Kirby's report: \"The substantial buying power of such an agency would strengthen the public prescription-drug insurance plans to negotiate the lowest possible purchase prices from drug companies.\""},{"key":"C","text":"What does \"national\" mean? Roy Romanow and Senator Michael Kirby recommended a federal-provincial body much like the recently created National Health Council."},{"key":"D","text":"The problem is simple and stark: health-care costs have been, are, and will continue to increase faster than government revenues."},{"key":"E","text":"According to the Canadian Institute for Health Information, prescription drug costs have risen since 1997 at twice the rate of overall health-care spending. Part of the increase comes from drugs being used to replace other kinds of treatments. Part of it arises from new drugs costing more than older kinds. Part of it is higher prices."},{"key":"F","text":"So, if the provinces want to run the health-care show, they should prove they can run it, starting with an interprovincial health list that would end duplication, save administrative costs, prevent one province from being played off against another, and bargain for better drug prices."},{"key":"G","text":"Of course, the pharmaceutical companies will scream. They like divided buyers; they can lobby better that way. They can use the threat of removing jobs from one province to another. They can hope that, if one province includes a drug on its list, the pressure will cause others to include it on theirs. They wouldn't like a national agency, but self-interest would lead them to deal with it."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"独占整段","stemZh":"42. (42)____","reason":"What does \"national\" mean? Roy Romanow and Senator Michael Kirby recommended a federal-provincial body much like the recently created National Health Council. \"国家\"是什么意思?Roy Romanow 和参议员 Michael Kirby 建议设立一个联邦-省联合机构,类似于最近成立的国家卫生委员会。\n下句‘national 不必是那样’需先有个‘那样’ 空格后第 6 段开头是 But \"national\" doesn't have to mean that——其中的 that 必须指向上一句给出的某种‘national 的含义’。可见这一空应当先抛出‘national 指什么’，并给出一种具体解释，供后句反驳。\n设问 national 并给一种解释 C 句 What does \"national\" mean 正面设问，并给出 a federal-provincial body 这一解释，恰好充当后句 that 的先行内容；第 6 段‘但 national 不必是那样（联邦—省级）、可以是省际’顺势反驳，缝合完美。","options":{},"choiceEn":"What does \"national\" mean? Roy Romanow and Senator Michael Kirby recommended a federal-provincial body much like the recently created National Health Council.","clues":["代词指代 that 指‘national 的一种含义’ doesn't have to mean that 的 that 在上文找不到先行词，必须由本空提供——本空要给出‘national=某种机构’的一个具体说法。","词汇复现 national 的复现与设问 后文反复出现 \"National\" could mean interprovincial，说明本空要正面处理‘national 一词的定义’，正确句应围绕 national 设问或定义。"]}},{"n":43,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Quebec's resistance to a national agency is provincialist ideology. One of the first advocates for a national list was a researcher at Laval University. Quebec's Drug Insurance Fund has seen its costs skyrocket with annual increases from 14.3 per cent to 26.8 per cent!"},{"key":"B","text":"Or they could read Mr. Kirby's report: \"The substantial buying power of such an agency would strengthen the public prescription-drug insurance plans to negotiate the lowest possible purchase prices from drug companies.\""},{"key":"C","text":"What does \"national\" mean? Roy Romanow and Senator Michael Kirby recommended a federal-provincial body much like the recently created National Health Council."},{"key":"D","text":"The problem is simple and stark: health-care costs have been, are, and will continue to increase faster than government revenues."},{"key":"E","text":"According to the Canadian Institute for Health Information, prescription drug costs have risen since 1997 at twice the rate of overall health-care spending. Part of the increase comes from drugs being used to replace other kinds of treatments. Part of it arises from new drugs costing more than older kinds. Part of it is higher prices."},{"key":"F","text":"So, if the provinces want to run the health-care show, they should prove they can run it, starting with an interprovincial health list that would end duplication, save administrative costs, prevent one province from being played off against another, and bargain for better drug prices."},{"key":"G","text":"Of course, the pharmaceutical companies will scream. They like divided buyers; they can lobby better that way. They can use the threat of removing jobs from one province to another. They can hope that, if one province includes a drug on its list, the pressure will cause others to include it on theirs. They wouldn't like a national agency, but self-interest would lead them to deal with it."}],"answer":"G","explanation":{"answer":"G","type":"独占整段","stemZh":"43. (43)____","reason":"Of course, the pharmaceutical companies will scream. They like divided buyers; they can lobby better that way. They can use the threat of removing jobs from one province to another. They can hope that, if one province includes a drug on its list, the pressure will cause others to include it on theirs. They wouldn't like a national agency, but self-interest would lead them to deal with it. 当然,制药公司会强烈反对。他们喜欢分散的购买者;这样他们可以更好地进行游说。他们可以利用将工作岗位从一个省转移到另一个省的威胁。他们可以希望,如果一个省将某种药物列入其名录,压力会导致其他省也将其列入名录。他们不会喜欢国家机构,但自身利益会促使他们与之打交道。\n上文刚论证‘集中议价压价’ 空格前第 7、8 段论证全国机构能代表更多人、议到更好价格（the national agency would negotiate on behalf of 31 million people）；空格后第 10 段说‘已朝全国机构迈出一小步’。这一空应承接‘集中议价’的话题，补上一个相关的反应或转折。\n药企对集中议价的强烈反应 G 句 Of course, the pharmaceutical companies will scream 让前文议价对手‘药企’登场，针对‘统一议价压价’作出可预见的反应，复现 drug 角色、逻辑顺承，且结尾‘自身利益会迫使其打交道’不妨碍下段另起‘一小步进展’。","options":{},"choiceEn":"Of course, the pharmaceutical companies will scream. They like divided buyers; they can lobby better that way. They can use the threat of removing jobs from one province to another. They can hope that, if one province includes a drug on its list, the pressure will cause others to include it on theirs. They wouldn't like a national agency, but self-interest would lead them to deal with it.","clues":["词汇复现 议价对象=drug manufacturers/药企 前文议价的对手是 negotiate better prices, if possible, with drug manufacturers，正确句应当出现‘药企’这一角色，并对‘集中议价’作出反应。","逻辑连接 Of course 引出意料中的反应 正确句以‘当然/Of course’这类口吻承接上文论证，引出可预见的对立反应，逻辑上正好嵌在‘论好处’与‘讲进展’之间。"]}},{"n":44,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Quebec's resistance to a national agency is provincialist ideology. One of the first advocates for a national list was a researcher at Laval University. Quebec's Drug Insurance Fund has seen its costs skyrocket with annual increases from 14.3 per cent to 26.8 per cent!"},{"key":"B","text":"Or they could read Mr. Kirby's report: \"The substantial buying power of such an agency would strengthen the public prescription-drug insurance plans to negotiate the lowest possible purchase prices from drug companies.\""},{"key":"C","text":"What does \"national\" mean? Roy Romanow and Senator Michael Kirby recommended a federal-provincial body much like the recently created National Health Council."},{"key":"D","text":"The problem is simple and stark: health-care costs have been, are, and will continue to increase faster than government revenues."},{"key":"E","text":"According to the Canadian Institute for Health Information, prescription drug costs have risen since 1997 at twice the rate of overall health-care spending. Part of the increase comes from drugs being used to replace other kinds of treatments. Part of it arises from new drugs costing more than older kinds. Part of it is higher prices."},{"key":"F","text":"So, if the provinces want to run the health-care show, they should prove they can run it, starting with an interprovincial health list that would end duplication, save administrative costs, prevent one province from being played off against another, and bargain for better drug prices."},{"key":"G","text":"Of course, the pharmaceutical companies will scream. They like divided buyers; they can lobby better that way. They can use the threat of removing jobs from one province to another. They can hope that, if one province includes a drug on its list, the pressure will cause others to include it on theirs. They wouldn't like a national agency, but self-interest would lead them to deal with it."}],"answer":"F","explanation":{"answer":"F","type":"独占整段","stemZh":"44. (44)____","reason":"So, if the provinces want to run the health-care show, they should prove they can run it, starting with an interprovincial health list that would end duplication, save administrative costs, prevent one province from being played off against another, and bargain for better drug prices. 因此,如果各省想要管理医疗保健事务,他们就应该证明自己能够管理好,首先从建立一个省际卫生名录开始,这将终结重复,节省行政成本,防止一个省被用来与另一个省对抗,并争取更优惠的药品价格。\n上文批省长只要钱、清单搁浅 空格前第 11 段批评几位省长只想要渥太华拨款、全国清单因此搁浅（the idea of a national list hasn't gone anywhere）；空格后第 13 段说省长们爱选择性引用报告。这一空应据前文省长的态度，作一个‘所以/那么’式的回应。\nSo 承前作结、复现清单 F 句 So, if the provinces want to run the health-care show, they should prove they can run it 顺着第 11 段省份的态度作结，复现 interprovincial health list，把‘搁浅的全国清单’落成‘那就先做跨省清单’，承上启下俱稳。","options":{},"choiceEn":"So, if the provinces want to run the health-care show, they should prove they can run it, starting with an interprovincial health list that would end duplication, save administrative costs, prevent one province from being played off against another, and bargain for better drug prices.","clues":["逻辑连接 由前文省份行为作结 空位前的第 11 段摆出省份的行为事实：They (particularly Quebec and Alberta) just want Ottawa to fork over additional billions with few, if any, strings attached. That's one reason why the idea of a national list hasn't gone anywhere, while drug costs keep rising fast. 空位独占第 12 段、紧跟其后，因此正确句应是对这一行为的收束——以表结论的承接词起头，由‘省份想要主导却又阻挠清单’推出‘那就得证明自己能管好’的要求。","词汇复现 复现 interprovincial / list 第 6 段已给出 National\" could mean interprovincial – provinces combining efforts to create one body.，第 11 段又以 the idea of a national list hasn't gone anywhere 收束；这要求正确句复现 interprovincial 与 list 这组词，把话题收回到‘跨省清单’这一可操作落点。"]}},{"n":45,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Quebec's resistance to a national agency is provincialist ideology. One of the first advocates for a national list was a researcher at Laval University. Quebec's Drug Insurance Fund has seen its costs skyrocket with annual increases from 14.3 per cent to 26.8 per cent!"},{"key":"B","text":"Or they could read Mr. Kirby's report: \"The substantial buying power of such an agency would strengthen the public prescription-drug insurance plans to negotiate the lowest possible purchase prices from drug companies.\""},{"key":"C","text":"What does \"national\" mean? Roy Romanow and Senator Michael Kirby recommended a federal-provincial body much like the recently created National Health Council."},{"key":"D","text":"The problem is simple and stark: health-care costs have been, are, and will continue to increase faster than government revenues."},{"key":"E","text":"According to the Canadian Institute for Health Information, prescription drug costs have risen since 1997 at twice the rate of overall health-care spending. Part of the increase comes from drugs being used to replace other kinds of treatments. Part of it arises from new drugs costing more than older kinds. Part of it is higher prices."},{"key":"F","text":"So, if the provinces want to run the health-care show, they should prove they can run it, starting with an interprovincial health list that would end duplication, save administrative costs, prevent one province from being played off against another, and bargain for better drug prices."},{"key":"G","text":"Of course, the pharmaceutical companies will scream. They like divided buyers; they can lobby better that way. They can use the threat of removing jobs from one province to another. They can hope that, if one province includes a drug on its list, the pressure will cause others to include it on theirs. They wouldn't like a national agency, but self-interest would lead them to deal with it."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"独占整段","stemZh":"45. (45)____","reason":"Or they could read Mr. Kirby's report: \"The substantial buying power of such an agency would strengthen the public prescription-drug insurance plans to negotiate the lowest possible purchase prices from drug companies.\" 或者他们可以阅读 Kirby 先生的报告:\"这样一个机构的巨大购买力将增强公共处方药保险计划与制药公司谈判以获得最低采购价格的能力。\"\n上句‘该读读罗马诺怎么说’ 空格前第 13 段说‘也许他们该读读罗马诺关于药品的话’并引了罗马诺的原话（Perhaps they should read what he had to say about drugs）；空格后第 15 段是结尾敦促行动。这一空应顺着‘该读读……’再补一个并列的‘或者还可以读读……’。\nOr + read…report 完美平行 B 句 Or they could read Mr. Kirby's report 与第 13 段‘该读读罗马诺的话’并列呼应，‘Or…read…report’句式与 read 复现双重对接；引柯比之言力挺全国机构，与下段‘敦促省长行动’衔接自然。","options":{},"choiceEn":"Or they could read Mr. Kirby's report: \"The substantial buying power of such an agency would strengthen the public prescription-drug insurance plans to negotiate the lowest possible purchase prices from drug companies.\"","clues":["逻辑连接 并列补充的第二项建议 空位前的第 13 段已给出一项建议：Perhaps they should read what he had to say about drugs 空位独占第 14 段、紧随其后，因此正确句应与之构成并列补充——以‘或者他们还可以读另一份报告’的并列结构起头，逻辑上必须紧接其后。","词汇复现 read / report 平行复现 前句 Perhaps they should read 的‘read 某人之言’在正确句里以 read Mr. Kirby's report 复现，形成‘读罗马诺→（或）读柯比’的平行，话题与句式双重呼应。"]}}]},{"id":"section2-part-c","kind":"translation","title":"Section II Part C 英译汉","directions":"Directions: Read the following text carefully and then translate the underlined segments into Chinese. Write your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. ( 10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-c-1","text":"It is not easy to talk about the role of the mass media in this overwhelmingly significant phase in European history. History and news become confused, and one's impressions tend to be a mixture of skepticism and optimism. (46) Television is one of the means by which these feelings are created and conveyed – and perhaps never before has it served so much to connect different peoples and nations as in the recent events in Europe. The Europe that is now forming cannot be anything other than its peoples, their cultures and national identities. With this in mind we can begin to analyze the European television scene. (47) In Europe, as elsewhere, multi-media groups have been increasingly successful; groups which bring together television, radio, newspapers, magazines and publishing houses that work in relation to one another. One Italian example would be the Berlusconi group, while abroad Maxwell and Murdoch come to mind."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-2","text":"Clearly, only the biggest and most flexible television companies are going to be able to compete in such a rich and hotly-contested market. (48) This alone demonstrates that the television business is not an easy world to survive in, a fact underlined by statistics that show that out of eighty European television networks, no less than 50% took a loss in 1989."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-3","text":"Moreover, the integration of the European community will oblige television companies to cooperate more closely in terms of both production and distribution."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-4","text":"(49) Creating a \"European identity\" that respects the different cultures and traditions which go to make up the connecting fabric of the Old Continent is no easy task and demands a strategic choice – that of producing programs in Europe for Europe. This entails reducing our dependence on the North American market, whose programs relate to experiences and cultural traditions which are different from our own."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-5","text":"In order to achieve these objectives, we must concentrate more on co-productions, the exchange of news, documentary services and training. This also involves the agreements between European countries for the creation of a European bank for Television Production which, on the model of the European Investments Bank, will handle the finances necessary for production costs. (50) In dealing with a challenge on such a scale, it is no exaggeration to say, \"United we stand, divided we fall\" – and if I had to choose a slogan it would be \"Unity in our diversity.\" A unity of objectives that nonetheless respects the varied peculiarities of each country."}]},"sentences":[{"n":46,"en":"Television is one of the means by which these feelings are created and conveyed – and perhaps never before has it served so much to connect different peoples and nations as in the recent events in Europe.","refZh":"电视是产生并传递这些（怀疑与乐观交织的）感受的手段之一——而且，或许在此之前，它从未像在近来这场欧洲变局中那样，如此有力地把不同的民族和国家连接到一起。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"Television is one of the means","lit":"电视是手段之一","role":"（主干主谓宾）","note":"难点： means 单复数同形 Television is one of the means by which these feelings are created and conveyed 中的 means 是「手段、方式」之意，虽以 s 结尾但单复数同形，这里 one of the means 直译为「众多手段中的一种」，不能误读成「意思」。"},{"en":"by which these feelings are created and conveyed","lit":"通过它，这些感受被创造和传递","role":"（which 引导的后置定语从句（修饰 means））","note":"难点： these feelings 必回指 + 被动转主动 these feelings 须回指上一句 one's impressions tend to be a mixture of skepticism and optimism，还原为「这些（怀疑与乐观交织的）感受」；are created and conveyed 被动语态在汉语里别硬翻成「被创造和被传递」，应转成主动或无主句「（人们）产生并传递这些感受」。"},{"en":"– and perhaps never before has it served so much to connect different peoples and nations","lit":"而且也许从未如此多地为连接不同的民族和国家服务过","role":"（并列分句 + 否定词倒装）","note":"难点： never before 引发完全倒装 + peoples 复数僻义 perhaps never before has it served so much to connect different peoples and nations 中 never before 置于句首引起完全倒装（has it served 而非 it has served），译时按正常语序还原为「它（电视）也许从未像……那样如此有力地……」。peoples 不是「人们」而是「（不同的）民族」，与 nations「国家」并列；这是熟词僻义，主体考生最易踩坑。it 回指 Television。"},{"en":"as in the recent events in Europe.","lit":"如同在欧洲近来发生的事件中那样","role":"（as 引导的比较状语（承接 never before…so much…as））","note":"难点： never before…so much…as 是同一比较句架 三段词其实是一个整体比较结构「never before…so much…as…」=「以前从未像……那样多／那样有力地……」。as 后省略了重复动词 it served；recent events in Europe 指当时欧洲一体化进程中的事件，译作「欧洲近期发生的事件／变局」即可。"}]},{"n":47,"en":"In Europe, as elsewhere, multi-media groups have been increasingly successful; groups which bring together television, radio, newspapers, magazines and publishing houses that work in relation to one another.","refZh":"在欧洲，正如在其他地方一样，多媒体集团变得越来越成功；这些集团把电视、广播、报纸、杂志和出版社整合到了一起，使它们相互联动、协同运作。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"In Europe, as elsewhere,","lit":"在欧洲，正如在其他地方一样，","role":"（地点状语 + 插入比较语）","note":"难点： as elsewhere 是省略比较 In Europe, as elsewhere, multi-media groups have been increasingly successful 中 as elsewhere 是 as (it is) elsewhere 的省略，意为「正如在其他地方那样」，说明这是全球现象而不只是欧洲。译时建议提到句首，与汉语习惯一致。"},{"en":"multi-media groups have been increasingly successful;","lit":"多媒体集团变得越来越成功；","role":"（主干主谓）","note":"难点： multi-media groups 是术语 multi-media groups 在本篇语境里指「跨电视、广播、报刊、出版的多媒体（传媒）集团」，最好统一译作「多媒体集团／跨媒体集团」，与下文 Berlusconi、Maxwell、Murdoch 这些媒体大亨案例对应。"},{"en":"groups which bring together television, radio, newspapers, magazines and publishing houses","lit":"这些集团把电视、广播、报纸、杂志和出版社汇集到一起","role":"（同位语 + which 引导定语从句（解释 groups 的构成））","note":"难点： bring together 的具体化 bring together 字面是「带到一起」，在本句指「把……整合／汇聚到同一集团旗下」，是企业并购语境下的常用动词；publishing houses 是「出版社」，不要直译成「出版的房子」。"},{"en":"that work in relation to one another.","lit":"（这些媒体）彼此关联地运作","role":"（嵌套定语从句（修饰 television…publishing houses 整体，说明它们彼此关联运作））","note":"难点： work in relation to one another 的内涵 in relation to one another = 彼此联动／相互关联，强调集团旗下各媒体不是各自为政、而是协同运作（资源、内容、品牌联动）。one another = each other = 彼此。考生易硬译为「与彼此相关地工作」，不通；改写为「相互联动／协同运作」更顺。"}]},{"n":48,"en":"This alone demonstrates that the television business is not an easy world to survive in, a fact underlined by statistics that show that out of eighty European television networks, no less than 50% took a loss in 1989.","refZh":"仅此一点就表明，电视业并非一个容易立足的行业，对此，有一组统计数据可以佐证——在 80 家欧洲电视网中，1989 年出现亏损的多达 50%。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"This alone demonstrates that the television business is not an easy world to survive in,","lit":"仅这一点就表明，电视业并不是一个容易生存其中的世界，","role":"（主句（主谓 + that 引导宾语从句））","note":"难点： This alone 必回指 + 抽象 world 具体化 This 须回指上一句 only the biggest and most flexible television companies are going to be able to compete in such a rich and hotly-contested market，整体可还原为「单是这种『只有最大最灵活的公司才能立足』的格局」；alone = 单单、仅仅这一点。an easy world to survive in 字面是「容易在其中生存的世界」，汉语应具体化为「容易立足／生存的行业」。"},{"en":"a fact underlined by statistics","lit":"这一事实被统计数据强调","role":"（同位语（解释前面整个 that 从句）+ 过去分词作后置定语（修饰 fact））","note":"难点： a fact 同位 + underlined 被动 a fact 是对前面整句「电视业难以生存」的同位概括，翻译时承接「这一事实／这一点」即可。underlined by statistics 字面「被统计数据加下划线」，underline 在此是「凸显／印证／佐证」的引申义（典型熟词僻义），不是「划线」。被动转主动：「这一事实被统计数据印证」→「统计数据印证了这一点」。"},{"en":"that show that out of eighty European television networks, no less than 50% took a loss in 1989.","lit":"（这些数据）显示，在 80 家欧洲电视网中，不少于 50% 在 1989 年出现亏损","role":"（that 引导定语从句修饰 statistics + 内嵌 that 引导宾语从句）","note":"难点： no less than 与 took a loss no less than 50% = 多达 50%／高达 50%／不少于 50%，带「数量之多令人意外」的语气，要把强调译出来。took a loss = 出现亏损／发生亏损，是商业术语；不要译作「带走一个损失」。television networks 在本文指「电视台／电视网络」，与上文 multi-media groups、television companies 同属传媒业语境。"}]},{"n":49,"en":"Creating a \"European identity\" that respects the different cultures and traditions which go to make up the connecting fabric of the Old Continent is no easy task and demands a strategic choice","refZh":"要打造这样一种「欧洲身份」——它既尊重各异的文化与传统，而这些文化与传统又正是把这块古老大陆（即欧洲）联结在一起的纽带——绝非易事，还需要做出一项战略抉择。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"Creating a \"European identity\"","lit":"打造一种「欧洲身份」","role":"（动名词短语作主语（核心））","note":"难点： 动名词作主语 + 引号保留 Creating… 是动名词短语作整句主语，谓语要到很靠后的 is no easy task 才出现，主体考生最容易在中间迷路。European identity 带引号属作者刻意标记，译文应保留引号译为「『欧洲身份』／『欧洲认同』」，呼应 its peoples, their cultures and national identities 中 identities 的语境。"},{"en":"that respects the different cultures and traditions","lit":"这种身份尊重不同的文化与传统","role":"（that 引导定语从句（修饰 European identity））","note":"难点： respect 取「尊重／顾及」之意 respect 在此不是「尊敬某人」，而是「尊重／顾及／不抹杀」不同文化传统的差异，对应下文 Unity in our diversity 的「多元中的统一」立场。"},{"en":"which go to make up the connecting fabric of the Old Continent","lit":"这些文化与传统共同构成了这块古老大陆的连接纽带","role":"（which 引导嵌套定语从句（修饰 cultures and traditions））","note":"难点： go to make up = 共同构成 + fabric 比喻 + Old Continent 别称 go to do 在此 = serve to do / help to do，「（共同）去做某事」，与 make up 连用 = 共同构成。connecting fabric 是比喻——fabric 字面「织物」，比喻「纽带／网络／肌理」；connecting fabric 即「把……联系起来的纽带／连接纹理」。Old Continent 是欧洲的别称「旧大陆／古老大陆」，可加注「欧洲」帮助理解。"},{"en":"is no easy task and demands a strategic choice","lit":"并非易事，并要求做出一个战略性选择","role":"（并列谓语（与主语 Creating… 搭配））","note":"难点： no easy task 否定加重 + strategic choice 政策语境 is no easy task 比 is not an easy task 语气更重，是「绝非易事／谈何容易」的语感。a strategic choice 在政策／战略语境里译作「一项战略性抉择／战略选择」，呼应下文破折号后 that of producing programs in Europe for Europe 给出的具体战略——「在欧洲为欧洲制作节目」。"}]},{"n":50,"en":"In dealing with a challenge on such a scale, it is no exaggeration to say, \"United we stand, divided we fall\"","refZh":"在应对如此规模的一项挑战时，可以毫不夸张地说：「团结则存，分裂则亡。」","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"In dealing with a challenge on such a scale,","lit":"在应对如此规模的一项挑战时，","role":"（介词短语作状语（条件／背景））","note":"难点： such a scale 必回指 + dealing with 取「应对」义 such a scale 须回指上文 Creating a \"European identity\" 与 we must concentrate more on co-productions, the exchange of news, documentary services and training 所说的欧洲电视一体化大业，可补译为「如此规模的挑战／如此重大的挑战」；dealing with 在此是「应对／处理／面对」，不是「与……交易」。"},{"en":"it is no exaggeration to say,","lit":"毫不夸张地说，","role":"（形式主语 it + 真实主语 to say（习惯结构））","note":"难点： it is no exaggeration to say 是固定句式 it is no exaggeration to say = 毫不夸张地说／可以毫不夸张地说，是英语里固定的让步式肯定句型。主体考生易犯错：把 no 与 exaggeration 分别直译成「不是一种夸大」之类的死板译法，影响通顺。"},{"en":"\"United we stand, divided we fall\"","lit":"「团结，我们就立；分裂，我们就倒」","role":"（直接引语（被引用的格言／谚语，作 say 的宾语））","note":"难点： United/divided 是过去分词作状语 + 谚语对仗 United we stand, divided we fall 是英语经典谚语，形式上 United 与 divided 都是过去分词作让步／条件状语，逻辑相当于「(When) united, we stand; (when) divided, we fall」。汉译要保留对仗工整、铿锵有力的口号感，常见译法有「团结则存，分裂则亡」「团结则立，分裂则倒」「合则立，分则败」。译文须用引号保留原句的「被引用」格式，呼应下一句作者自拟的口号 Unity in our diversity.。"}]}]},{"id":"section3-part-a","kind":"writing","title":"Section III Part A 写作","directions":"51. Directions: Two months ago you got a job as an editor for the magazine Designs & Fashions. But now you find that the work is not what you expected. You decide to quit. Write a letter to your boss, Mr. Wang, telling him your decision, stating your reason(s), and making an apology. Write your letter with no less than 100 words. Write it neatly on ANSWER SHEET 2. Do not sign your own name at the end of the letter; use “Li Ming” instead. You do not need to write the address. (10 points)","prompt":"51. Directions: Two months ago you got a job as an editor for the magazine Designs & Fashions. But now you find that the work is not what you expected. You decide to quit. Write a letter to your boss, Mr. Wang, telling him your decision, stating your reason(s), and making an apology. Write your letter with no less than 100 words. Write it neatly on ANSWER SHEET 2. Do not sign your own name at the end of the letter; use “Li Ming” instead. You do not need to write the address. (10 points)","points":10,"image":null,"model":{"essay":"Dear Mr. Wang, I am writing to inform you of my decision to resign from my position as an editor for the magazine Designs & Fashions. I have worked here for two months, and I regret to say that the job has turned out to be quite different from what I expected. When I applied, I hoped to focus on creative editing, but most of my time is now spent on routine paperwork that does not suit my interests or my career plans. After careful thought, I have decided to leave and pursue a position that fits me better. Please accept my sincere apology for any inconvenience my leaving may cause. I am truly grateful for the opportunity you offered me and for your kind guidance during these two months. I will do my best to hand over my work smoothly before I go. Yours sincerely, Li Ming","translation":"尊敬的王先生：\n我写信是想告知您，我决定辞去在《设计与时尚》杂志社担任编辑的职务。\n我已在此工作两个月，很遗憾地说，这份工作与我此前的预期颇为不同。当初应聘时，我希望能专注于创意编辑，但如今大部分时间都花在与我的兴趣和职业规划都不相符的事务性工作上。经过慎重考虑，我决定离开，去寻找一个更适合自己的岗位。\n请接受我诚挚的歉意，为我的离职可能带来的任何不便。我真心感谢您给予我的这个机会，以及这两个月来您的悉心指导。离开之前，我会尽力做好工作交接。\n此致 李明","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"称呼对位上级","text":"【第 1 段】 称呼对位上级 。Dear Mr. Wang, 直接对位收信人身份，用 Mr.+姓，落实了 outline 第一块『称呼』并定下正式语域。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"首句即点辞职","text":"【第 2 段】 首句即点辞职 。用句架 I am writing to inform you of my decision to resign… 一句话开门见山亮出辞职决定，兼带职位，完成告知任务。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"对比式理由","text":"【第 3 段】 对比式理由 。the job has turned out to be quite different from what I expected 加上 When I applied, I hoped… but… 的前后对比，把理由写得具体可信，撑满一段。"},{"tag":"【第 4 段】","core":"先歉后谢","text":"【第 4 段】 先歉后谢 。Please accept my sincere apology… 完成致歉，随后 I am truly grateful for… 致谢并承诺交接，落实道歉任务并中和负面色彩。"},{"tag":"【第 5 段】","core":"敬语加指定署名","text":"【第 5 段】 敬语加指定署名 。Yours sincerely, 换行 Li Ming，用指定署名收尾，守住格式分，未写真名、未写地址。"}],"wordCount":146}},{"id":"section3-part-b","kind":"writing","title":"Section III Part B 写作","directions":"52. Directions: Write an essay of 160 – 200 words based on the following drawing. In your essay, you should first describe the drawing, then interpret its meaning, and give your comment on it. You should write neatly on ANSWER SHEET 2. (20 points)","prompt":"52. Directions: Write an essay of 160 – 200 words based on the following drawing. In your essay, you should first describe the drawing, then interpret its meaning, and give your comment on it. You should write neatly on ANSWER SHEET 2. (20 points)","points":20,"image":{"src":"assets/writing/2005-section3-part-b.jpg","alt":"这是一幅黑白漫画，标题是“养老‘足球赛’”","width":"818","height":"830","local":"assets/writing/2005-section3-part-b.jpg"},"model":{"essay":"As is vividly depicted in the cartoon, four adult children, namely the eldest son, the second son, the third son and the youngest daughter, each stand before a goal at the four corners of a field. In the center, an elderly parent is bent into the shape of a ball and kicked back and forth among them, like a so-called 'football match' of elderly care. Simple as the picture is, it conveys a thought-provoking message. The old man kicked around like a ball symbolizes aging parents whom no child is willing to support; instead of shouldering their duty, the siblings push the responsibility onto one another. What the artist satirizes is the shameful truth that some children, though many, treat their parents as a burden to shove away rather than a duty to honor. In my view, such behavior deserves severe condemnation, for it violates the basic virtue of filial piety. Supporting one's parents is not only a moral obligation but also a legal duty. Therefore, every child ought to take the initiative to care for the aged, and society should strengthen moral education so that no parent ends up as a 'ball' in this heartless game.","translation":"如漫画生动描绘的那样，四个成年子女——大儿子、二儿子、三儿子和小女儿——分别站在场地四角的球门前。场地中央，一位老人弯着腰变成了球的形状，在他们之间被踢来踢去，宛如一场所谓的养老\"足球赛\"。\n画面虽简单，却传递出发人深省的信息。那位像球一样被踢来踢去的老人，象征着无人愿意赡养的年迈父母；子女们非但不担起责任，反而把责任互相推给对方。画家所讽刺的，正是这样一个可耻的事实：一些子女虽然人数众多，却把父母当作要甩开的负担，而非应当尽的孝道。\n在我看来，这种行为应受到严厉谴责，因为它违背了孝道这一基本美德。赡养父母不仅是道德义务，也是法律责任。因此，每个子女都应主动承担起照顾老人的责任，社会也应加强道德教育，使任何父母都不至于沦为这场冷漠游戏里被踢的\"球\"。","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"现在时白描画面","text":"【第 1 段】 现在时白描画面 。用 'As is vividly depicted in the cartoon' 总起，随后按'四个子女守门→中央老人被踢'的顺序客观呈现，全部用现在时（stand、is bent、kicked），不掺评价，正好落实第一段'描述画面'的功能。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"一一对应再点破","text":"【第 2 段】 一一对应再点破 。先用 'Simple as the picture is, it conveys a thought-provoking message' 从画面过渡到寓意，再用 'symbolizes' 把被踢的老人对应到无人赡养的父母，最后 'What the artist satirizes is...' 点破讽刺主旨，把第二段做透，兑现寓意这一得分核心。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"表态给理由再对策","text":"【第 3 段】 表态给理由再对策 。'In my view... for it violates...' 亮态度并给理由，'Therefore, every child ought to... and society should...' 从个人与社会两层给对策，结尾 'no parent ends up as a ball' 回扣画面，完成评论收尾。"}],"wordCount":197}}],"questionCount":45,"objectiveCount":45,"answerTable":{"ans-section1":"完形填空（四选一） Section I （1–20 题） 1 C but · 2 B unlike · 3 A limited · 4 C missing · 5 B though · 6 A even if · 7 D detecting · 8 A diluted · 9 D whereas · 10 B particular · 11 C messages · 12 A at first · 13 D exposed · 14 C inefficient · 15 D create · 16 B also · 17 C aware · 18 D notice · 19 A available · 20 B such as \n（逐题解析见 完形填空（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-1":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (1) （21–25 题） 21 C · 22 B · 23 A · 24 C · 25 B \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-2":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (2) （26–30 题） 26 C · 27 D · 28 A · 29 D · 30 B \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-3":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (3) （31–35 题） 31 A · 32 C · 33 D · 34 D · 35 A \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-4":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (4) （36–40 题） 36 B · 37 D · 38 A · 39 B · 40 C \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-b":"选句填空（7选5） Section II Part B （41–45 题） 41 E · 42 C · 43 G · 44 F · 45 B \n（逐题解析见 选句填空（7选5）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-c":"英译汉（划线句子翻译） Section II Part C \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 英译汉（划线句子翻译）解析页","ans-section3-part-a":"写作 Section III Part A \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页","ans-section3-part-b":"写作 Section III Part B \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页"},"coverage":{"objective":45,"explained":45,"missingSections":[]}};
